  "contracts": {
    "lem-periodic-fourier-mode-aliasing-under-sampling": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-periodic-fourier-collocation-grid",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $N\\ge2$ be an integer, let $x_j=2\\pi j/N$ for $j=0,\\ldots,N-1$, and identify $x_N$ with $x_0$ periodically. The real trigonometric interpolation space is\n$$\n\\mathcal T_N=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\operatorname{span}_{\\mathbb R}\\{1,\\cos(kx),\\sin(kx):1\\le k\\le (N-1)/2\\},&N\\text{ odd},\\\\\n\\operatorname{span}_{\\mathbb R}\\{1,\\cos(kx),\\sin(kx):1\\le k<N/2\\}\\oplus\\operatorname{span}_{\\mathbb R}\\{\\cos(Nx/2)\\},&N\\text{ even}.\n\\end{cases}\n$$\nFor real nodal data $U=(U_0,\\ldots,U_{N-1})$, define its periodic Fourier interpolant by\n$$\nI_NU(x)=\\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}U_jL_j(x),\n$$\nwhere, for $N=2K+1$,\n$$\nL_j(x)=\\frac1N\\left(1+2\\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\cos(k(x-x_j))\\right),\n$$\nand, for $N=2K$,\n$$\nL_j(x)=\\frac1N\\left(1+2\\sum_{k=1}^{K-1}\\cos(k(x-x_j))+\\cos(K(x-x_j))\\right).\n$$\nThus the even-grid Nyquist component is the real cosine $\\cos(Nx/2)$ only. Its sampled derivative is defined by differentiating this interpolant; the resulting Nyquist derivative values are zero at every grid point.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-complex-exponential-addition-and-real-extension",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all $z,w\\in\\mathbb C$, $\\exp(z+w)=\\exp z\\exp w$. For real $x$, the complex value $\\exp(x+0i)$ equals the published real exponential $e^x$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[def-complex-exponential]], [[lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere]], [[lem-cauchy-product-of-absolutely-convergent-complex-series]], [[def-real-exponential-function-and-e]], [[lem-binomial-theorem-over-complex-numbers]], [[thm-binomial-closed-formula]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-kernel-and-fibres-of-complex-exponential",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "$$\\ker(\\exp)=2\\pi i\\mathbb Z,$$\nand $\\exp z=\\exp w$ exactly when $z-w\\in2\\pi i\\mathbb Z$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity]], [[thm-exponential-is-strictly-increasing]], [[thm-sine-cosine-zero-sets-and-fundamental-period]], [[thm-sine-and-cosine-parametrize-the-unit-circle]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-finite-fourier-inversion",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $N\\ge1$ and $f\\in\\mathbb C^{\\mathbb Z/N}$. Then for every $x\\in\\mathbb Z/N\\mathbb Z$\n\n$$f(x)=N^{-1/2}\\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}(\\mathcal F_Nf)(k)\\,e^{2\\pi ikx/N},$$\n\nthe right-hand side being independent of the chosen integer representative of the class $x$. Consequently $\\mathcal F_N$ is bijective with inverse the positive-sign transform\n\n$$(\\mathcal G_Ng)(x):=N^{-1/2}\\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}g(k)\\,e^{2\\pi ikx/N},\\qquad g\\in\\mathbb C^{\\mathbb Z/N},\\ x\\in\\mathbb Z/N\\mathbb Z,$$\n\nand there is no convergence, regularity or support hypothesis anywhere.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Since $k-r=qN$, for every grid index $j$ we have $e^{i(k-r)x_j}=e^{2\\pi iqj}=1$ by [F2]. Multiplying by $e^{irx_j}$ gives $e^{ikx_j}=e^{irx_j}$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For frequencies $k_1,\\ldots,k_s$, multiplication of the sampled modes gives $\\prod_{\\ell=1}^s e^{ik_\\ell x_j}=e^{i(\\sum_\\ell k_\\ell)x_j}$ by the exponential addition law in [F2]. Applying step 1.1 to this sum and any congruent representative proves the product aliasing identity.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For the summed frequency, reduce modulo $N$ to the representative in the residue set defining $\\mathcal T_N$. Step 1.1 gives the same samples for this representative, and [F3] makes its interpolant unique. This is a statement about the sampled product and makes no claim that its continuous product equals the representative mode away from the grid.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
