    "thm-periodic-fourier-collocation-differentiation-is-exact": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-periodic-fourier-collocation-grid",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $N\\ge2$ be an integer, let $x_j=2\\pi j/N$ for $j=0,\\ldots,N-1$, and identify $x_N$ with $x_0$ periodically. The real trigonometric interpolation space is\n$$\n\\mathcal T_N=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\operatorname{span}_{\\mathbb R}\\{1,\\cos(kx),\\sin(kx):1\\le k\\le (N-1)/2\\},&N\\text{ odd},\\\\\n\\operatorname{span}_{\\mathbb R}\\{1,\\cos(kx),\\sin(kx):1\\le k<N/2\\}\\oplus\\operatorname{span}_{\\mathbb R}\\{\\cos(Nx/2)\\},&N\\text{ even}.\n\\end{cases}\n$$\nFor real nodal data $U=(U_0,\\ldots,U_{N-1})$, define its periodic Fourier interpolant by\n$$\nI_NU(x)=\\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}U_jL_j(x),\n$$\nwhere, for $N=2K+1$,\n$$\nL_j(x)=\\frac1N\\left(1+2\\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\cos(k(x-x_j))\\right),\n$$\nand, for $N=2K$,\n$$\nL_j(x)=\\frac1N\\left(1+2\\sum_{k=1}^{K-1}\\cos(k(x-x_j))+\\cos(K(x-x_j))\\right).\n$$\nThus the even-grid Nyquist component is the real cosine $\\cos(Nx/2)$ only. Its sampled derivative is defined by differentiating this interpolant; the resulting Nyquist derivative values are zero at every grid point.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "alg-periodic-fourier-collocation-differentiation-matrix",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Input an integer $N\\ge2$ and real data $U_0,\\ldots,U_{N-1}$ on the grid of [[def-periodic-fourier-collocation-grid]]. Set $K=(N-1)/2$ when $N$ is odd and $K=N/2$ when $N$ is even. Form the $(N\\times N)$ matrix\n$$\n(D_N)_{ij}=\n\\begin{cases}\n-\\dfrac2N\\displaystyle\\sum_{k=1}^{K}k\\sin(k(x_i-x_j)),&N\\text{ odd},\\\\[1.2ex]\n-\\dfrac2N\\displaystyle\\sum_{k=1}^{K-1}k\\sin(k(x_i-x_j)),&N\\text{ even},\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad 0\\le i,j<N.\n$$\nThe algorithm returns $D_N$ and the derivative vector $D_NU$. In the even case it omits the derivative of the Nyquist cosine because $\\sin((N/2)(x_i-x_j))=0$ for grid indices $i,j$. All operations are in exact real arithmetic; no floating-point stability or error guarantee is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-orthogonality-of-characters-on-a-finite-cyclic-group",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $N\\ge1$ and let $k,\\ell\\in\\mathbb Z$. Then\n\n$$\\sum_{x=0}^{N-1}e^{2\\pi i(k-\\ell)x/N}=\\begin{cases}N,&k\\equiv\\ell\\pmod N,\\\\\\\\ 0,&k\\not\\equiv\\ell\\pmod N.\\end{cases}$$\n\nAt $N=1$ the congruence holds for all $k,\\ell$ and the sum is $1$. The sum is the finite sum of the complex family $x\\mapsto e^{2\\pi i(k-\\ell)x/N}$ over the von Neumann natural $N=\\{0,\\dots,N-1\\}$ ([[def-finite-sum-in-a-commutative-monoid]]), and $N$ on the right is the natural number $N$ read in $\\mathbb C$ as the additive multiple $N\\cdot1_{\\mathbb C}$, that is, the value at $N$ of the canonical embedding $\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb C$ ([[def-canonical-natural]], [[lem-integer-multiples-agree-with-canonical-natural]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-finite-fourier-inversion",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $N\\ge1$ and $f\\in\\mathbb C^{\\mathbb Z/N}$. Then for every $x\\in\\mathbb Z/N\\mathbb Z$\n\n$$f(x)=N^{-1/2}\\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}(\\mathcal F_Nf)(k)\\,e^{2\\pi ikx/N},$$\n\nthe right-hand side being independent of the chosen integer representative of the class $x$. Consequently $\\mathcal F_N$ is bijective with inverse the positive-sign transform\n\n$$(\\mathcal G_Ng)(x):=N^{-1/2}\\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}g(k)\\,e^{2\\pi ikx/N},\\qquad g\\in\\mathbb C^{\\mathbb Z/N},\\ x\\in\\mathbb Z/N\\mathbb Z,$$\n\nand there is no convergence, regularity or support hypothesis anywhere.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The functions $\\sin$ and $\\cos$ are differentiable on $\\mathbb R$, with\n$$(\\sin x)'=\\cos x,\\qquad(\\cos x)'=-\\sin x.$$\nAlso $\\sin0=0$ and $\\cos0=1$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For real $x,y$, $\\exp(x+iy)=e^x(\\cos y+i\\sin y)$ and $|\\exp(x+iy)|=e^x$. In particular $e^{i\\pi}+1=0$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[thm-complex-exponential-addition-and-real-extension]], [[thm-eulers-formula]], [[lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws]], [[thm-pythagorean-and-parity-identities-for-all-six-trigonometric-functions]], [[cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity]], [[thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas]], [[def-pi-via-first-positive-cosine-zero]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$,\n$$\\sin(x+\\pi/2)=\\cos x,\\quad\\cos(x+\\pi/2)=-\\sin x,\\quad\\sin(x+\\pi)=-\\sin x,\\quad\\cos(x+\\pi)=-\\cos x.$$\nIn particular,\n$$\\sin(\\pi/2)=1,\\quad\\cos(\\pi/2)=0,\\quad\\sin\\pi=0,\\quad\\cos\\pi=-1.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-sine-and-cosine-subtraction-formulas",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all real $u,v$,\n$$\\sin(u-v)=\\sin u\\cos v-\\cos u\\sin v,\\qquad \\cos(u-v)=\\cos u\\cos v+\\sin u\\sin v.$$ The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[thm-sine-and-cosine-addition-formulas]], [[cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A, B \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with\n$g[A] \\subseteq B$ and let $f : B \\to \\mathbb{R}$, so that the composite\n$f \\circ g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is defined. Let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of\n$A$ ([[def-limit-point-r]]) at which $g$ is differentiable\n([[def-derivative]]), put $b := g(c)$, and suppose $b$ is a limit point of $B$\nat which $f$ is differentiable. Then $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $c$ and\n\n$$(f \\circ g)'(c) \\;=\\; f'\\bigl(g(c)\\bigr)\\,g'(c) .$$\n\n**Both limit-point hypotheses are needed, and neither is automatic.** That $c$\nis a limit point of $A$ is what makes $g'(c)$ and $(f \\circ g)'(c)$ defined\nsymbols; that $b = g(c)$ is a limit point of $B$ is what makes $f'(b)$ one.\nNothing forces the second: $g$ may be differentiable at $c$ and send $c$ to an\nisolated point of $B$, and there $f'(b)$ is not defined and the formula asserts\nnothing.\n\n**No case analysis appears anywhere.** The naive difference-quotient proof\nwrites $\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(c))}{g(x) - g(c)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(c)}{x - c}$\nand then has to say what happens where $g(x) = g(c)$, which may occur at points\narbitrarily close to $c$. Carathéodory's factorisation never divides by the\ninner increment, so the difficulty does not arise.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "By [F8], each low-frequency cosine difference in $L_j$ expands into the corresponding cosine/sine modes in $\\mathcal T_N$. For even $N=2K$, its Nyquist term expands into $\\cos(Kx)\\cos(Kx_j)+\\sin(Kx)\\sin(Kx_j)$; [F7] makes the second coefficient zero because $Kx_j=\\pi j$. At a grid difference $x_i-x_j=2\\pi(i-j)/N$, Euler's identity [F6] rewrites the cosine sum defining $L_j(x_i)$ as the normalized sum of all characters in a complete residue system modulo $N$: for odd $N=2K+1$ the residues are $-K,\\ldots,K$, and for even $N=2K$ they are $-K+1,\\ldots,K$. Character orthogonality [F3] gives $L_j(x_i)=1$ when $i=j$ and $0$ otherwise. Thus $I_NU$ takes the prescribed nodal values and lies in $\\mathcal T_N$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "The space $\\mathcal T_N$ has exactly $N$ real basis modes. If two members agree at the $N$ nodes, their difference has zero nodal Fourier transform; inversion [F4] forces every frequency coefficient to vanish, so the interpolant is unique.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Differentiate the finite sum $p(x)=\\sum_jU_jL_j(x)$ term by term. By [F5] and [F9], $p'(x_i)=\\sum_jU_jL_j'(x_i)$. When $N$ is odd this is exactly the sine sum defining $D_NU$ in [F2]. When $N=2K$ the derivative of the remaining Nyquist term is proportional to $\\sin(K(x_i-x_j))=\\sin(\\pi(i-j))=0$ by [F7], so the same equality holds with the Nyquist term omitted.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "F7",
            "F9",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Steps 1.1–2.1 establish the nodal interpolation and derivative identity for every data vector, including both parity branches of $N$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
