    "lem-chebyshev-lobatto-mode-aliasing": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-chebyshev-gauss-lobatto-collocation-grid",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $N\\ge1$ be an integer and put $x_j=\\cos(j\\pi/N)$ for $j=0,\\ldots,N$. The ordering is decreasing: $x_0=1$ and $x_N=-1$. These are the Chebyshev–Gauss–Lobatto nodes; [[thm-chebyshev-multiple-angle-identities]] gives $T_N(x_j)=(-1)^j$. They are distinct because cosine is strictly decreasing on $[0,\\pi]$ by [[thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges]].\n\nFor interpolation using [[def-cardinal-polynomial-interpolation]], use the increasing reindexing $z_k=x_{N-k}$, $k=0,\\ldots,N$. For descending-grid data $U_j$, the corresponding increasing data are $Y_k=U_{N-k}$.\n\nFor nodal data $U_0,\\ldots,U_N$, define $I_N^LU$ to be the cardinal interpolant of $(Y_k)_{k=0}^N$ at $(z_k)_{k=0}^N$. For a function $f$ defined on the interval, write $I_N^Lf$ for the interpolant of its nodal values. The increasing-node interpolation theorem [[thm-cardinal-polynomial-interpolant-exists-uniquely]] makes this polynomial unique.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-chebyshev-polynomials-first-and-second-kind",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal P_{\\mathbb R}$ be the set of formal real polynomials of\n[[def-real-polynomial-degree-leading-coefficient-and-monic]]. Associate to every\n$P\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathbb R}$ an eventually zero coefficient sequence $(p_k)$:\nuse its displayed finite coefficient list and extend it by zeros when $P\\ne0$,\nand put $p_k=0$ for every $k$ when $P=0$. Do the same for $Q$, with coefficients\n$(q_k)$. Define $P+Q$ coefficientwise and define $PQ$ by the convolution\ncoefficients\n$$ (P+Q)_k=p_k+q_k,\\qquad (PQ)_k=\\sum_{j=0}^{k}p_jq_{k-j}. $$\nusing the real finite sum of [[def-finite-sum]]. In either construction, discard\ntrailing zero coefficients; if every coefficient is zero, the result is the zero\npolynomial. Scalar multiplication and subtraction are the corresponding\ncoefficientwise operations. Thus these formulas define operations on the formal\ncoefficient-list objects, independently of evaluation.\n\nWrite $1=(1)$ and $X=(0,1)$, and define\n$$ \\Phi(P,Q):=(Q,2XQ-P)\\qquad(P,Q\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathbb R}). $$\nApply [[thm-recursion]] to the set\n$\\mathcal P_{\\mathbb R}\\times\\mathcal P_{\\mathbb R}$, first with initial value\n$(1,X)$ and then with initial value $(1,2X)$, always using the function $\\Phi$.\nThis gives unique pair sequences $H^T,H^U$. Define $T_n$ and $U_n$ to be the\nfirst coordinates of $H^T_n$ and $H^U_n$, respectively. Since the first\ncoordinate of $\\Phi(P,Q)$ is $Q$, the second coordinate of $H^T_n$ is\n$T_{n+1}$ and the second coordinate of $H^U_n$ is $U_{n+1}$. Consequently\n$$ T_0=1,\\quad T_1=X,\\quad T_{n+2}=2XT_{n+1}-T_n, $$\n$$ U_0=1,\\quad U_1=2X,\\quad U_{n+2}=2XU_{n+1}-U_n $$\nfor every $n\\in\\mathbb N$. These unique sequences are the **Chebyshev\npolynomials of the first and second kinds**.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-chebyshev-multiple-angle-identities",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every $n\\in\\mathbb N$ and real $\\theta$,\n$$T_n(\\cos\\theta)=\\cos(n\\theta),\\qquad U_n(\\cos\\theta)\\sin\\theta=\\sin((n+1)\\theta).$$ In particular, for $n\\ge1$ and $0\\le j\\le n$, $T_n(\\cos(j\\pi/n))=(-1)^j$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[def-chebyshev-polynomials-first-and-second-kind]], [[thm-sine-and-cosine-addition-formulas]], [[cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity]], [[thm-induction-principle]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-cardinal-polynomial-interpolant-exists-uniquely",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\in\\mathbb N$, $n\\ge0$, let $x_0<\\cdots<x_n$ be distinct real nodes, and let $y_0,\\ldots,y_n\\in\\mathbb R$. Then $I_n y$ from [[def-cardinal-polynomial-interpolation]] is the unique real polynomial of degree at most $n$ satisfying $p(x_i)=y_i$ for every $i=0,\\ldots,n$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-chebyshev-degree-leading-coefficients-and-extreme-values",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $n\\ge1$, $T_n$ has degree $n$ and leading coefficient $2^{n-1}$, while $U_n$ has degree $n$ and leading coefficient $2^n$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[def-chebyshev-polynomials-first-and-second-kind]], [[thm-induction-principle]], [[def-integer-power]].",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$,\n$$\\sin(x+\\pi/2)=\\cos x,\\quad\\cos(x+\\pi/2)=-\\sin x,\\quad\\sin(x+\\pi)=-\\sin x,\\quad\\cos(x+\\pi)=-\\cos x.$$\nIn particular,\n$$\\sin(\\pi/2)=1,\\quad\\cos(\\pi/2)=0,\\quad\\sin\\pi=0,\\quad\\cos\\pi=-1.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Reduce $n$ modulo $2N$ to a residue $s\\in\\{0,\\ldots,2N-1\\}$ and put $r=\\min(s,2N-s)$. Then $0\\le r\\le N$, $r\\equiv n$ or $-n\\pmod{2N}$, and this $r$ is unique: the classes $\\{\\pm r\\}$ are distinct for $0<r<N$, with singleton classes at $r=0,N$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "choose",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For every node $x_j$, [F2] gives $T_n(x_j)=\\cos(nj\\pi/N)$. Since $n\\equiv\\pm r\\pmod{2N}$, [F5] gives $\\cos(nj\\pi/N)=\\cos(rj\\pi/N)=T_r(x_j)$. Thus $T_n$ and $T_r$ have the same nodal values.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "By [F4], $T_r$ has degree $r\\le N$. Interpolation uniqueness [F3] therefore gives $I_N^LT_n=T_r$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
