    "thm-chebyshev-gauss-lobatto-differentiation-is-exact": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-chebyshev-gauss-lobatto-collocation-grid",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $N\\ge1$ be an integer and put $x_j=\\cos(j\\pi/N)$ for $j=0,\\ldots,N$. The ordering is decreasing: $x_0=1$ and $x_N=-1$. These are the Chebyshev–Gauss–Lobatto nodes; [[thm-chebyshev-multiple-angle-identities]] gives $T_N(x_j)=(-1)^j$. They are distinct because cosine is strictly decreasing on $[0,\\pi]$ by [[thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges]].\n\nFor interpolation using [[def-cardinal-polynomial-interpolation]], use the increasing reindexing $z_k=x_{N-k}$, $k=0,\\ldots,N$. For descending-grid data $U_j$, the corresponding increasing data are $Y_k=U_{N-k}$.\n\nFor nodal data $U_0,\\ldots,U_N$, define $I_N^LU$ to be the cardinal interpolant of $(Y_k)_{k=0}^N$ at $(z_k)_{k=0}^N$. For a function $f$ defined on the interval, write $I_N^Lf$ for the interpolant of its nodal values. The increasing-node interpolation theorem [[thm-cardinal-polynomial-interpolant-exists-uniquely]] makes this polynomial unique.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "alg-chebyshev-gauss-lobatto-differentiation-matrix",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Input $N\\ge1$ and real nodal values $U_0,\\ldots,U_N$ at the descending grid $x_j=\\cos(j\\pi/N)$ from [[def-chebyshev-gauss-lobatto-collocation-grid]]. Set $z_k=x_{N-k}$ and $Y_k=U_{N-k}$, so $z_0<\\cdots<z_N$. Form the cardinal polynomials $\\ell_k$ for the increasing nodes $z_0,\\ldots,z_N$ by [[def-cardinal-polynomial-interpolation]]. Define\n$$\n(D_N)_{ij}=\\ell_{N-j}'(x_i),\\qquad 0\\le i,j\\le N,\n$$\nand return the derivative vector $D_NU$. Equivalently, form $p(x)=\\sum_{k=0}^NY_k\\ell_k(x)$ and return $(p'(x_i))_{i=0}^N$. The procedure is exact-real arithmetic and makes no stability claim for a floating-point implementation.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-cardinal-polynomial-interpolant-exists-uniquely",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\in\\mathbb N$, $n\\ge0$, let $x_0<\\cdots<x_n$ be distinct real nodes, and let $y_0,\\ldots,y_n\\in\\mathbb R$. Then $I_n y$ from [[def-cardinal-polynomial-interpolation]] is the unique real polynomial of degree at most $n$ satisfying $p(x_i)=y_i$ for every $i=0,\\ldots,n$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Reindex the data by $Y_k=U_{N-k}$. By [F1], the nodes $z_k=x_{N-k}$ are strictly increasing, so [F3] gives $p(x)=\\sum_{k=0}^NY_k\\ell_k(x)$ as the unique degree-at-most-$N$ interpolant. Reversing the finite sum back to the descending index gives $p(x)=\\sum_{j=0}^NU_j\\ell_{N-j}(x)$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Differentiate the finite polynomial sum in step 1.1 term by term. At $x_i$ this gives $p'(x_i)=\\sum_{j=0}^NU_j\\ell_{N-j}'(x_i)=\\sum_{j=0}^N(D_N)_{ij}U_j=(D_NU)_i$ by [F2]. This proves the first identity for each node.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The polynomial $p'$ has degree at most $N-1\\le N$ and its nodal values are $D_NU$ by step 2.1. Uniqueness [F3] therefore makes $p'$ the interpolant of the vector $D_NU$. Applying the already proved first-derivative identity of step 2.1 to that vector gives $D_N(D_NU)=(p''(x_i))_{i=0}^N$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "thm-chebyshev-gauss-lobatto-differentiation-is-exact has no empty-set or empty-interval branch beyond its explicit hypotheses."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "thm-chebyshev-gauss-lobatto-differentiation-is-exact excludes a zero size/degree by its stated lower bound, or has no zero parameter."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement includes the one-index case; the general calculation in step 1.1 applies at that value."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "thm-chebyshev-gauss-lobatto-differentiation-is-exact has no additional degenerate interval or collapsed-grid branch beyond its stated hypotheses."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The statement and step 3.1 quantify over every grid index, including the first and last nodes."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement and steps 1.1 and 2.2 use explicit finite data or theorem-provided witnesses; the axiom_use field records the applicable choice branch."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "thm-chebyshev-gauss-lobatto-differentiation-is-exact states a one-way estimate, identity, exactness claim or worked computation, not an iff."
