    "lem-chebyshev-polynomials-orthogonal-in-arcsine-weight": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-chebyshev-arcsine-weighted-l2-pairing",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For continuous real functions $f,g$ on $[-1,1]$, define\n$$\n\\langle f,g\\rangle_C:=\\int_0^\\pi f(\\cos\\theta)g(\\cos\\theta)\\,d\\theta,\\qquad \\|f\\|_C^2:=\\langle f,f\\rangle_C.\n$$\nThis is the arcsine-weighted pairing on the compact Chebyshev interval: in the variable $x=\\cos\\theta$ its weight is $\\omega_C(x)=(1-x^2)^{-1/2}$ for $-1<x<1$. The angular integral is the endpoint convention, so no endpoint value of the singular density is required.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-chebyshev-multiple-angle-identities",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every $n\\in\\mathbb N$ and real $\\theta$,\n$$T_n(\\cos\\theta)=\\cos(n\\theta),\\qquad U_n(\\cos\\theta)\\sin\\theta=\\sin((n+1)\\theta).$$ In particular, for $n\\ge1$ and $0\\le j\\le n$, $T_n(\\cos(j\\pi/n))=(-1)^j$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[def-chebyshev-polynomials-first-and-second-kind]], [[thm-sine-and-cosine-addition-formulas]], [[cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity]], [[thm-induction-principle]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-sine-and-cosine-addition-formulas",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all real $x,y$,\n$$\\sin(x+y)=\\sin x\\cos y+\\cos x\\sin y,$$\n$$\\cos(x+y)=\\cos x\\cos y-\\sin x\\sin y.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The functions $\\sin$ and $\\cos$ are differentiable on $\\mathbb R$, with\n$$(\\sin x)'=\\cos x,\\qquad(\\cos x)'=-\\sin x.$$\nAlso $\\sin0=0$ and $\\cos0=1$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-ftc-second-part",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a < b$ be reals, let $G : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable at every\npoint of $[a,b]$ as a function on $[a,b]$ ([[def-derivative]]; at $a$ and $b$\nthis is the one-sided derivative), let $f := G'$, and suppose $f$ is integrable\non $[a,b]$ ([[def-darboux-integral]]). Then\n\n$$\\int_a^b f \\;=\\; G(b) - G(a) .$$\n\n**Both hypotheses are needed and neither is removable.** A function may be\ndifferentiable everywhere with $G'$ not integrable — then the left-hand side does\nnot exist (an everywhere differentiable function with unbounded derivative) — and an\nintegrable $f$ need not be the derivative of anything\n(the sign function); both witnesses are on the\ncompanion page.\n\n**No continuity of $f$ is assumed**, which is what makes this the working form:\nthe theorem evaluates $\\int_a^b f$ for every integrable derivative, not only for\ncontinuous integrands.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$,\n$$\\sin(x+\\pi/2)=\\cos x,\\quad\\cos(x+\\pi/2)=-\\sin x,\\quad\\sin(x+\\pi)=-\\sin x,\\quad\\cos(x+\\pi)=-\\cos x.$$\nIn particular,\n$$\\sin(\\pi/2)=1,\\quad\\cos(\\pi/2)=0,\\quad\\sin\\pi=0,\\quad\\cos\\pi=-1.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$,\n$$\\sin(-x)=-\\sin x,\\qquad\\cos(-x)=\\cos x,\\qquad\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x=1.$$\nConsequently $|\\sin x|\\le1$ and $|\\cos x|\\le1$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-chebyshev-polynomials-first-and-second-kind",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal P_{\\mathbb R}$ be the set of formal real polynomials of\n[[def-real-polynomial-degree-leading-coefficient-and-monic]]. Associate to every\n$P\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathbb R}$ an eventually zero coefficient sequence $(p_k)$:\nuse its displayed finite coefficient list and extend it by zeros when $P\\ne0$,\nand put $p_k=0$ for every $k$ when $P=0$. Do the same for $Q$, with coefficients\n$(q_k)$. Define $P+Q$ coefficientwise and define $PQ$ by the convolution\ncoefficients\n$$ (P+Q)_k=p_k+q_k,\\qquad (PQ)_k=\\sum_{j=0}^{k}p_jq_{k-j}. $$\nusing the real finite sum of [[def-finite-sum]]. In either construction, discard\ntrailing zero coefficients; if every coefficient is zero, the result is the zero\npolynomial. Scalar multiplication and subtraction are the corresponding\ncoefficientwise operations. Thus these formulas define operations on the formal\ncoefficient-list objects, independently of evaluation.\n\nWrite $1=(1)$ and $X=(0,1)$, and define\n$$ \\Phi(P,Q):=(Q,2XQ-P)\\qquad(P,Q\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathbb R}). $$\nApply [[thm-recursion]] to the set\n$\\mathcal P_{\\mathbb R}\\times\\mathcal P_{\\mathbb R}$, first with initial value\n$(1,X)$ and then with initial value $(1,2X)$, always using the function $\\Phi$.\nThis gives unique pair sequences $H^T,H^U$. Define $T_n$ and $U_n$ to be the\nfirst coordinates of $H^T_n$ and $H^U_n$, respectively. Since the first\ncoordinate of $\\Phi(P,Q)$ is $Q$, the second coordinate of $H^T_n$ is\n$T_{n+1}$ and the second coordinate of $H^U_n$ is $U_{n+1}$. Consequently\n$$ T_0=1,\\quad T_1=X,\\quad T_{n+2}=2XT_{n+1}-T_n, $$\n$$ U_0=1,\\quad U_1=2X,\\quad U_{n+2}=2XU_{n+1}-U_n $$\nfor every $n\\in\\mathbb N$. These unique sequences are the **Chebyshev\npolynomials of the first and second kinds**.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "cor-weierstrass-approximation-on-a-closed-interval",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $a\\le b$, every continuous real function on $[a,b]$ is a uniform limit of polynomials.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-heine-borel-r",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a \\le b$. Then the closed bounded interval\n$[a,b]$ ([[def-interval]]) is compact ([[def-open-cover-r]]): every family of\nopen subsets of $\\mathbb{R}$ whose union contains $[a,b]$ has a finite subfamily\nwhose union already contains $[a,b]$.\n\nThe proof is by repeated bisection. Supposing some open cover admits no finite\nsubcover, one halves the interval, keeps a half that still admits none, and\niterates; the halves shrink to a point, which the cover does reach, and a single\nmember of the cover then swallows a whole late-stage half. **The halving rule is\ncanonical**, taking the left half whenever the left half works, so the recursion\nuses [[thm-recursion]] and no choice principle.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-extreme-value-r",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $f : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be continuous on $A$\n([[def-continuity-real]]) and let $K \\subseteq A$ be **nonempty** and compact\n([[def-open-cover-r]]). Then $\\sup f[K]$ and $\\inf f[K]$ exist and are\n**attained**: there are $p, q \\in K$ with\n\n$$f(q) \\;=\\; \\inf f[K] \\;\\le\\; f(x) \\;\\le\\; \\sup f[K] \\;=\\; f(p) \\qquad \\text{for every } x \\in K .$$\n\nEquivalently, the set $f[K]$ has a maximum and a minimum\n([[def-max-min]]), namely $\\max f[K] = f(p)$ and $\\min f[K] = f(q)$.\n\n**Nonemptiness of $K$ is a hypothesis, not an oversight.** For $K = \\varnothing$\nthe set $f[K]$ is empty, and neither a supremum nor a maximum of the empty set\nexists in this library ([[def-complete-ordered-field]] supplies suprema of\nnonempty sets bounded above only).\n\n**This theorem is stated twice in this library, on purpose.** Its metric-space\ntwin is [[thm-extreme-value-metric]], proved from the cover machinery of metric\nspaces; the proof below is $\\mathbb{R}$-native, running through Heine-Borel for\n$\\mathbb{R}$ and the order-completeness of $\\mathbb{R}$, and it uses no cover\nargument beyond the one already spent in\n[[thm-continuous-image-of-a-compact-set-r]]. That the two statements are the\nsame statement in two vocabularies is proved in\n[[lem-real-and-metric-notions-agree]], later on this page.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-monotonicity-of-the-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a < b$ be reals and let $f, g : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be integrable\n([[def-darboux-integral]]). Then:\n\n1. **Nonnegativity.** If $f(x) \\ge 0$ for every $x \\in [a,b]$ then\n   $\\int_a^b f \\ge 0$.\n2. **Monotonicity.** If $f(x) \\le g(x)$ for every $x \\in [a,b]$ then\n   $$\\int_a^b f \\;\\le\\; \\int_a^b g .$$\n3. **Two-sided bound.** If $m \\le f(x) \\le M$ for every $x \\in [a,b]$, with\n   $m, M$ real, then\n   $$m\\,(b-a) \\;\\le\\; \\int_a^b f \\;\\le\\; M\\,(b-a) .$$\n\n**Equality in claim 1 does not force $f$ to vanish.** A nonnegative integrable\nfunction with integral $0$ may be positive at infinitely many points; that is\n[[fs-nonnegative-integrable-with-zero-integral-vanishes]] on the previous page's\ncompanion. Under the additional hypothesis of continuity the conclusion does\nhold, and that is [[thm-nonnegative-continuous-with-zero-integral-vanishes]]\nbelow.\n\n**Claim 2 is stated for $a < b$ and is not orientation-invariant.** With the\nconvention of [[def-oriented-integral]], $f \\le g$ gives\n$\\int_u^v f \\le \\int_u^v g$ when $u \\le v$ and the reverse inequality when\n$u \\ge v$, since both sides change sign together.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "By [F1]–[F2], $\\langle T_m,T_n\\rangle_C=\\int_0^\\pi\\cos(m\\theta)\\cos(n\\theta)\\,d\\theta$. If $m\\ne n$, [F3], cosine parity [F5], and $\\sin(k\\pi)=0$ from [F5] make the integral of each nonzero-frequency cosine vanish by [F4]. If $m=n\\ge1$, [F3] gives $\\frac12(1+\\cos(2m\\theta))$, whose integral is $\\pi/2$. If $m=n=0$, the integrand is $1$ and its integral is $\\pi$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The recurrence [F6] shows inductively that $T_n$ has degree $n$ and nonzero leading coefficient: this holds for $T_0,T_1$, and the leading term of $2XT_n$ cannot cancel with the lower-degree $T_{n-1}$. Eliminating the leading term of any polynomial successively proves that $T_0,T_1,\\ldots$ span all polynomials; their distinct degrees also give linear independence. Step 1.1 gives positive squared norms and pairwise orthogonality.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F6",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra",
            "induction"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Suppose continuous $h$ pairs to zero with every $T_n$. By step 2.1 it pairs to zero with every polynomial. Fix $\\varepsilon>0$ and choose a polynomial $q$ with $\\|q-h\\|_\\infty<\\varepsilon$ by [F7]. The bound $M=\\|h\\|_\\infty<\\infty$ follows from [F8]. The pointwise bounds $-M\\varepsilon\\le h(\\cos\\theta)(h-q)(\\cos\\theta)\\le M\\varepsilon$ and [F9] give $0\\le\\langle h,h\\rangle_C=\\langle h,h-q\\rangle_C\\le\\pi M\\varepsilon.$ The lower inequality uses the nonnegative integrand $h(\\cos\\theta)^2$ and [F9]. Since this holds for every $\\varepsilon>0$, $\\langle h,h\\rangle_C=0$. If $h(x_0)\\ne0$ at some $x_0\\in[-1,1]$, choose $\\theta_0\\in[0,\\pi]$ with $\\cos\\theta_0=x_0$; continuity makes $h(\\cos\\theta)^2$ bounded below by a positive constant on a one-sided neighborhood if $\\theta_0$ is an endpoint, or a two-sided neighborhood otherwise. [F9] then gives a positive integral, contradicting zero norm. Therefore $h=0$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "step 2.1",
            "choose",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "For any continuous $h$, if $\\langle h,h\\rangle_C=0$ but $h(\\cos\\theta_0)\\ne0$ at some $\\theta_0\\in[0,\\pi]$, continuity gives a one-sided interval at an endpoint, or a two-sided interval in the interior, on which $h(\\cos\\theta)^2$ is bounded below by a positive number. [F9] makes its integral positive, a contradiction. Thus $h(\\cos\\theta)=0$ for every $\\theta$, and cosine maps $[0,\\pi]$ onto $[-1,1]$, so $h=0$. Symmetry and bilinearity follow from the integral definition [F1], proving positive definiteness.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F9",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "lem-chebyshev-polynomials-orthogonal-in-arcsine-weight has no empty-set or empty-interval branch beyond its explicit hypotheses."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
