    },
    "thm-chebyshev-weighted-orthogonal-projection-rate": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-chebyshev-weighted-spectral-sobolev-scale",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $s\\ge0$, define $H_C^s$ to be the completion of finite Chebyshev sums $p=\\sum_{n=0}^{M}c_nT_n$ in the norm\n$$\n\\|p\\|_{H_C^s}^2=\\pi|c_0|^2+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\sum_{n=1}^{M}(1+n^2)^s|c_n|^2.\n$$\nBy [[lem-chebyshev-polynomials-orthogonal-in-arcsine-weight]], the coefficients of a finite sum are unique and this is a norm. The underlying $L_C^2$ norm is the arcsine-weighted norm from [[def-chebyshev-arcsine-weighted-l2-pairing]]. This is the spectral Sobolev scale for the named weight and domain; it is defined by Chebyshev coefficients, and no equivalence with an unweighted derivative-based Sobolev norm is asserted.\n\nFor $u=\\sum_{n\\ge0}c_nT_n$ in this completion, define the degree-$N$ orthogonal projection by\n$$\n\\Pi_Nu=\\sum_{n=0}^{N}c_nT_n.\n$$\nThe coefficient series and the projection are well-defined by the orthogonality and positive coefficient norm above.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-chebyshev-polynomials-orthogonal-in-arcsine-weight",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Under the pairing of [[def-chebyshev-arcsine-weighted-l2-pairing]],\n$$\n\\langle T_m,T_n\\rangle_C=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\pi,&m=n=0,\\\\\n\\pi/2,&m=n\\ge1,\\\\\n0,&m\\ne n.\n\\end{cases}\n$$\nThe polynomials $T_0,T_1,\\ldots$ form a basis of the real polynomial space, and the pairing is positive definite on continuous real functions on $[-1,1]$. If a continuous real function $h$ satisfies $\\langle h,T_n\\rangle_C=0$ for every $n\\ge0$, then $h=0$ on $[-1,1]$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "By [F1], $u-\\Pi_Nu=\\sum_{n>N}c_nT_n$. Orthogonality from [F2] gives $\\|u-\\Pi_Nu\\|_C^2=\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\sum_{n>N}|c_n|^2.$ Since $n>N$ implies $(1+n^2)^s\\ge(1+(N+1)^2)^s$, this is at most $(1+(N+1)^2)^{-s}\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\sum_{n>N}(1+n^2)^s|c_n|^2 \\le(1+(N+1)^2)^{-s}\\|u\\|_{H_C^s}^2.$ Taking square roots proves the result for finite sums.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Finite sums are dense in the completion defining $H_C^s$. The truncation $\\Pi_N$ is a contraction in the coefficient norm and in the $C$-norm, so both sides of the finite-sum inequality pass to the limit along any Cauchy sequence of finite sums. This yields the stated estimate for every $u\\in H_C^s$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "thm-chebyshev-weighted-orthogonal-projection-rate has no empty-set or empty-interval branch beyond its explicit hypotheses."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement includes a zero mode/index, handled in step 1.1."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "thm-chebyshev-weighted-orthogonal-projection-rate has no separate one-size regime beyond the stated parameter range."
