    "thm-chebyshev-lobatto-interpolation-rate-under-spectral-sobolev-regularity": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-chebyshev-weighted-spectral-sobolev-scale",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $s\\ge0$, define $H_C^s$ to be the completion of finite Chebyshev sums $p=\\sum_{n=0}^{M}c_nT_n$ in the norm\n$$\n\\|p\\|_{H_C^s}^2=\\pi|c_0|^2+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\sum_{n=1}^{M}(1+n^2)^s|c_n|^2.\n$$\nBy [[lem-chebyshev-polynomials-orthogonal-in-arcsine-weight]], the coefficients of a finite sum are unique and this is a norm. The underlying $L_C^2$ norm is the arcsine-weighted norm from [[def-chebyshev-arcsine-weighted-l2-pairing]]. This is the spectral Sobolev scale for the named weight and domain; it is defined by Chebyshev coefficients, and no equivalence with an unweighted derivative-based Sobolev norm is asserted.\n\nFor $u=\\sum_{n\\ge0}c_nT_n$ in this completion, define the degree-$N$ orthogonal projection by\n$$\n\\Pi_Nu=\\sum_{n=0}^{N}c_nT_n.\n$$\nThe coefficient series and the projection are well-defined by the orthogonality and positive coefficient norm above.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-chebyshev-lobatto-mode-aliasing",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $N\\ge1$, let $x_j=\\cos(j\\pi/N)$, and let $I_N^L$ denote degree-at-most-$N$ interpolation on these nodes. For every integer $n\\ge0$, let $\\rho_N(n)$ be the unique integer $r\\in\\{0,\\ldots,N\\}$ congruent to either $n$ or $-n$ modulo $2N$. Then\n$$\nI_N^L T_n=T_{\\rho_N(n)}.\n$$\nIn particular, sampling a Chebyshev series on the Lobatto grid can fold every degree $n>N$ to a degree at most $N$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-cardinal-polynomial-interpolation",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n\\in\\mathbb N$ with $n\\ge0$, let $x_0<\\cdots<x_n$ be distinct real nodes, and let $y_0,\\ldots,y_n\\in\\mathbb R$. Define the node polynomial and barycentric weights by\n$$\\omega_n(X)=\\prod_{k=0}^{n}(X-x_k),\\qquad w_j=\\left(\\prod_{\\substack{0\\le k\\le n\\\\k\\ne j}}(x_j-x_k)\\right)^{-1}\\quad(0\\le j\\le n).$$\nAn empty product is $1$, so $w_0=1$ when $n=0$.\n\nIn these formulas, $X$ is a real argument, and for real $a$ and nonzero real $b$, $a/b$ denotes $ab^{-1}$ in the field $\\mathbb R$ ([[def-field]]).\n\nFor each $j$, the cardinal function is\n$$\\ell_j(X)=\\prod_{\\substack{0\\le k\\le n\\\\k\\ne j}}\\frac{X-x_k}{x_j-x_k}.$$\nThe interpolation function for the data $y=(y_0,\\ldots,y_n)$ is\n$$I_n y(X)=\\sum_{j=0}^{n}y_j\\ell_j(X).$$\nFor a real function $f$ defined at the nodes, write $I_n f:=I_n(f(x_0),\\ldots,f(x_n))$.\n\nFor products indexed by $k\\ne j$, use the length-$n$ list $\\widehat x_{j,k}$ ($0\\le k<n$), where $\\widehat x_{j,k}=x_k$ for $k<j$ and $\\widehat x_{j,k}=x_{k+1}$ for $k\\ge j$; the product is in this order and is empty when $n=0$. Strict ordering gives $x_j-\\widehat x_{j,k}\\ne0$ for every factor, so [[lem-finite-product-of-nonzero-reals-is-nonzero]] and the field inverse law make each $w_j$ well defined.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-chebyshev-polynomials-orthogonal-in-arcsine-weight",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Under the pairing of [[def-chebyshev-arcsine-weighted-l2-pairing]],\n$$\n\\langle T_m,T_n\\rangle_C=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\pi,&m=n=0,\\\\\n\\pi/2,&m=n\\ge1,\\\\\n0,&m\\ne n.\n\\end{cases}\n$$\nThe polynomials $T_0,T_1,\\ldots$ form a basis of the real polynomial space, and the pairing is positive definite on continuous real functions on $[-1,1]$. If a continuous real function $h$ satisfies $\\langle h,T_n\\rangle_C=0$ for every $n\\ge0$, then $h=0$ on $[-1,1]$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-chebyshev-multiple-angle-identities",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every $n\\in\\mathbb N$ and real $\\theta$,\n$$T_n(\\cos\\theta)=\\cos(n\\theta),\\qquad U_n(\\cos\\theta)\\sin\\theta=\\sin((n+1)\\theta).$$ In particular, for $n\\ge1$ and $0\\le j\\le n$, $T_n(\\cos(j\\pi/n))=(-1)^j$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[def-chebyshev-polynomials-first-and-second-kind]], [[thm-sine-and-cosine-addition-formulas]], [[cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity]], [[thm-induction-principle]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Sine is strictly increasing on each interval $[-\\pi/2+2m\\pi,\\pi/2+2m\\pi]$ and strictly decreasing on each interval $[\\pi/2+2m\\pi,3\\pi/2+2m\\pi]$. Cosine is strictly decreasing on $[2m\\pi,(2m+1)\\pi]$ and strictly increasing on $[(2m+1)\\pi,(2m+2)\\pi]$. Both functions have range $[-1,1]$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-ltwo-fourier-decay-implies-absolute-convergence-by-cauchy-schwarz",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $s>1/2$. If a sequence $(a_k)$ satisfies $\\sum_k(1+|k|)^{2s}|a_k|^2<\\infty$, then $(a_k)\\in\\ell^1(\\mathbb Z)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-cauchy-schwarz-finite",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ and let $a_0, \\dots, a_{n-1}$ and $b_0, \\dots, b_{n-1}$ be\nreals, with finite sums as in [[def-finite-sum]]. Then\n\n$$\\Big(\\sum_{k<n} a_k b_k\\Big)^{2} \\;\\le\\; \\Big(\\sum_{k<n} a_k^{2}\\Big)\\Big(\\sum_{k<n} b_k^{2}\\Big),$$\n\nand, in root form ([[thm-of-square-roots]]),\n\n$$\\Big|\\sum_{k<n} a_k b_k\\Big| \\;\\le\\; \\sqrt{\\sum_{k<n} a_k^{2}} \\; \\sqrt{\\sum_{k<n} b_k^{2}}.$$\n\nEquality holds in the first display if and only if the two lists are\nproportional, in the symmetric sense that there is a pair\n$(\\lambda, \\mu) \\ne (0,0)$ of reals with $\\lambda a_k = \\mu b_k$ for every\n$k < n$.\n\n**No root is used in the proof of the squared form.** That form is an identity\nplus a sign argument in the ordered field, and the root form is only a\nrestatement of it through the monotonicity of squaring on the nonnegatives\n([[lem-of-square-monotone]]); the root enters nowhere but the last step, where\nthat restatement is made and [[thm-of-square-roots]] is what supplies the\nsquare-root symbol. This matters here, because it makes the squared\ninequality independent of the existence theorem for roots.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Put $a_n=c_n$ for $n\\ge0$ and $a_n=0$ for negative $n$. By [F1], $\\sum_{n\\ge0}(1+n^2)^s|c_n|^2<\\infty$; since $(1+n)^{2s}\\le2^s(1+n^2)^s$, [F6] gives $\\sum_{n\\ge0}|c_n|<\\infty$. By [F5], $|T_n(x)|\\le1$ on $[-1,1]$, so the series converges uniformly and its nodal values are defined.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Write $u=S_N+R_N$, where $S_N=\\sum_{n=0}^Nc_nT_n$ and $R_N=\\sum_{n>N}c_nT_n$. The Lagrange operator [F3] reproduces $S_N$ and is continuous in the uniform norm; therefore $u-I_N^Lu=R_N-I_N^LR_N$. By [F2], uniform convergence and the finite cardinal sum give $I_N^LR_N=\\sum_{r=0}^Nb_rT_r$, where $b_r=\\sum_{n>N:\\rho_N(n)=r}c_n$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Fix $r\\in\\{0,\\ldots,N\\}$. Since $n\\equiv r$ or $-r\\pmod{2N}$, every index in its alias class has form $2qN+r$ or $2qN-r$. When $n>N$ the corresponding integer $q$ is positive. For each $q$ there are at most two candidates; the plus candidate is at least $qN$, and the minus candidate is at least $(2q-1)N\\ge qN$. Hence $\\sum_{n>N:\\rho_N(n)=r}(1+n^2)^{-s}\\le2N^{-2s}\\sum_{q\\ge1}q^{-2s}\\le2\\left(1+\\frac1{2s-1}\\right)N^{-2s}.$ The last inequality separates the $q=1$ term and bounds the decreasing tail by $\\int_1^\\infty x^{-2s}\\,dx$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Let $A_s=1+1/(2s-1)$. Apply [F7] to finite partial sums over an alias class, use step 3.1, and pass to the limit; the coefficient sums converge absolutely by step 1.1. This gives $|b_r|^2\\le2A_sN^{-2s}\\sum_{n>N:\\rho_N(n)=r}(1+n^2)^s|c_n|^2.$ The alias classes partition $n>N$. By [F4], $\\|I_N^LR_N\\|_C^2\\le\\pi\\sum_{r=0}^N|b_r|^2\\le2\\pi A_sN^{-2s}\\sum_{n>N}(1+n^2)^s|c_n|^2\\le4A_sN^{-2s}\\|u\\|_{H_C^s}^2,$ where the last inequality uses [F1]. Also [F1] and [F4] give $\\|R_N\\|_C^2=\\frac\\pi2\\sum_{n>N}|c_n|^2\\le(N+1)^{-2s}\\|u\\|_{H_C^s}^2.$ Thus $\\|R_N\\|_C\\le(N+1)^{-s}\\|u\\|_{H_C^s}$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F7",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "By step 2.1 and the triangle inequality, $\\|u-I_N^Lu\\|_C\\le\\|R_N\\|_C+\\|I_N^LR_N\\|_C\\le(1+2\\sqrt{A_s})N^{-s}\\|u\\|_{H_C^s}$ because $(N+1)^{-s}\\le N^{-s}$. This is the claimed estimate.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
