    },
    "thm-chebyshev-lobatto-pointwise-remainder-bound": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-chebyshev-gauss-lobatto-collocation-grid",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $N\\ge1$ be an integer and put $x_j=\\cos(j\\pi/N)$ for $j=0,\\ldots,N$. The ordering is decreasing: $x_0=1$ and $x_N=-1$. These are the Chebyshev–Gauss–Lobatto nodes; [[thm-chebyshev-multiple-angle-identities]] gives $T_N(x_j)=(-1)^j$. They are distinct because cosine is strictly decreasing on $[0,\\pi]$ by [[thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges]].\n\nFor interpolation using [[def-cardinal-polynomial-interpolation]], use the increasing reindexing $z_k=x_{N-k}$, $k=0,\\ldots,N$. For descending-grid data $U_j$, the corresponding increasing data are $Y_k=U_{N-k}$.\n\nFor nodal data $U_0,\\ldots,U_N$, define $I_N^LU$ to be the cardinal interpolant of $(Y_k)_{k=0}^N$ at $(z_k)_{k=0}^N$. For a function $f$ defined on the interval, write $I_N^Lf$ for the interpolant of its nodal values. The increasing-node interpolation theorem [[thm-cardinal-polynomial-interpolant-exists-uniquely]] makes this polynomial unique.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-chebyshev-polynomials-first-and-second-kind",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal P_{\\mathbb R}$ be the set of formal real polynomials of\n[[def-real-polynomial-degree-leading-coefficient-and-monic]]. Associate to every\n$P\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathbb R}$ an eventually zero coefficient sequence $(p_k)$:\nuse its displayed finite coefficient list and extend it by zeros when $P\\ne0$,\nand put $p_k=0$ for every $k$ when $P=0$. Do the same for $Q$, with coefficients\n$(q_k)$. Define $P+Q$ coefficientwise and define $PQ$ by the convolution\ncoefficients\n$$ (P+Q)_k=p_k+q_k,\\qquad (PQ)_k=\\sum_{j=0}^{k}p_jq_{k-j}. $$\nusing the real finite sum of [[def-finite-sum]]. In either construction, discard\ntrailing zero coefficients; if every coefficient is zero, the result is the zero\npolynomial. Scalar multiplication and subtraction are the corresponding\ncoefficientwise operations. Thus these formulas define operations on the formal\ncoefficient-list objects, independently of evaluation.\n\nWrite $1=(1)$ and $X=(0,1)$, and define\n$$ \\Phi(P,Q):=(Q,2XQ-P)\\qquad(P,Q\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathbb R}). $$\nApply [[thm-recursion]] to the set\n$\\mathcal P_{\\mathbb R}\\times\\mathcal P_{\\mathbb R}$, first with initial value\n$(1,X)$ and then with initial value $(1,2X)$, always using the function $\\Phi$.\nThis gives unique pair sequences $H^T,H^U$. Define $T_n$ and $U_n$ to be the\nfirst coordinates of $H^T_n$ and $H^U_n$, respectively. Since the first\ncoordinate of $\\Phi(P,Q)$ is $Q$, the second coordinate of $H^T_n$ is\n$T_{n+1}$ and the second coordinate of $H^U_n$ is $U_{n+1}$. Consequently\n$$ T_0=1,\\quad T_1=X,\\quad T_{n+2}=2XT_{n+1}-T_n, $$\n$$ U_0=1,\\quad U_1=2X,\\quad U_{n+2}=2XU_{n+1}-U_n $$\nfor every $n\\in\\mathbb N$. These unique sequences are the **Chebyshev\npolynomials of the first and second kinds**.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-chebyshev-degree-leading-coefficients-and-extreme-values",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $n\\ge1$, $T_n$ has degree $n$ and leading coefficient $2^{n-1}$, while $U_n$ has degree $n$ and leading coefficient $2^n$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[def-chebyshev-polynomials-first-and-second-kind]], [[thm-induction-principle]], [[def-integer-power]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-chebyshev-multiple-angle-identities",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every $n\\in\\mathbb N$ and real $\\theta$,\n$$T_n(\\cos\\theta)=\\cos(n\\theta),\\qquad U_n(\\cos\\theta)\\sin\\theta=\\sin((n+1)\\theta).$$ In particular, for $n\\ge1$ and $0\\le j\\le n$, $T_n(\\cos(j\\pi/n))=(-1)^j$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[def-chebyshev-polynomials-first-and-second-kind]], [[thm-sine-and-cosine-addition-formulas]], [[cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity]], [[thm-induction-principle]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A, B \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with\n$g[A] \\subseteq B$ and let $f : B \\to \\mathbb{R}$, so that the composite\n$f \\circ g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is defined. Let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of\n$A$ ([[def-limit-point-r]]) at which $g$ is differentiable\n([[def-derivative]]), put $b := g(c)$, and suppose $b$ is a limit point of $B$\nat which $f$ is differentiable. Then $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $c$ and\n\n$$(f \\circ g)'(c) \\;=\\; f'\\bigl(g(c)\\bigr)\\,g'(c) .$$\n\n**Both limit-point hypotheses are needed, and neither is automatic.** That $c$\nis a limit point of $A$ is what makes $g'(c)$ and $(f \\circ g)'(c)$ defined\nsymbols; that $b = g(c)$ is a limit point of $B$ is what makes $f'(b)$ one.\nNothing forces the second: $g$ may be differentiable at $c$ and send $c$ to an\nisolated point of $B$, and there $f'(b)$ is not defined and the formula asserts\nnothing.\n\n**No case analysis appears anywhere.** The naive difference-quotient proof\nwrites $\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(c))}{g(x) - g(c)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(c)}{x - c}$\nand then has to say what happens where $g(x) = g(c)$, which may occur at points\narbitrarily close to $c$. Carathéodory's factorisation never divides by the\ninner increment, so the difficulty does not arise.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The functions $\\sin$ and $\\cos$ are differentiable on $\\mathbb R$, with\n$$(\\sin x)'=\\cos x,\\qquad(\\cos x)'=-\\sin x.$$\nAlso $\\sin0=0$ and $\\cos0=1$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$,\n$$\\sin(x+\\pi/2)=\\cos x,\\quad\\cos(x+\\pi/2)=-\\sin x,\\quad\\sin(x+\\pi)=-\\sin x,\\quad\\cos(x+\\pi)=-\\cos x.$$\nIn particular,\n$$\\sin(\\pi/2)=1,\\quad\\cos(\\pi/2)=0,\\quad\\sin\\pi=0,\\quad\\cos\\pi=-1.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$,\n$$\\sin(-x)=-\\sin x,\\qquad\\cos(-x)=\\cos x,\\qquad\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x=1.$$\nConsequently $|\\sin x|\\le1$ and $|\\cos x|\\le1$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-cardinal-polynomial-interpolation",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n\\in\\mathbb N$ with $n\\ge0$, let $x_0<\\cdots<x_n$ be distinct real nodes, and let $y_0,\\ldots,y_n\\in\\mathbb R$. Define the node polynomial and barycentric weights by\n$$\\omega_n(X)=\\prod_{k=0}^{n}(X-x_k),\\qquad w_j=\\left(\\prod_{\\substack{0\\le k\\le n\\\\k\\ne j}}(x_j-x_k)\\right)^{-1}\\quad(0\\le j\\le n).$$\nAn empty product is $1$, so $w_0=1$ when $n=0$.\n\nIn these formulas, $X$ is a real argument, and for real $a$ and nonzero real $b$, $a/b$ denotes $ab^{-1}$ in the field $\\mathbb R$ ([[def-field]]).\n\nFor each $j$, the cardinal function is\n$$\\ell_j(X)=\\prod_{\\substack{0\\le k\\le n\\\\k\\ne j}}\\frac{X-x_k}{x_j-x_k}.$$\nThe interpolation function for the data $y=(y_0,\\ldots,y_n)$ is\n$$I_n y(X)=\\sum_{j=0}^{n}y_j\\ell_j(X).$$\nFor a real function $f$ defined at the nodes, write $I_n f:=I_n(f(x_0),\\ldots,f(x_n))$.\n\nFor products indexed by $k\\ne j$, use the length-$n$ list $\\widehat x_{j,k}$ ($0\\le k<n$), where $\\widehat x_{j,k}=x_k$ for $k<j$ and $\\widehat x_{j,k}=x_{k+1}$ for $k\\ge j$; the product is in this order and is empty when $n=0$. Strict ordering gives $x_j-\\widehat x_{j,k}\\ne0$ for every factor, so [[lem-finite-product-of-nonzero-reals-is-nonzero]] and the field inverse law make each $w_j$ well defined.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-lagrange-interpolation-remainder",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\in\\mathbb N$, $n\\ge0$, let $a<b$, let $x_0<\\cdots<x_n$ lie in $[a,b]$, and let $f\\in C^{n+1}([a,b],\\mathbb R)$ with the convention of [[def-higher-derivatives-and-smoothness]]. Put $p=I_n f$ and $\\omega_n(X)=\\prod_{j=0}^n(X-x_j)$ as in [[def-cardinal-polynomial-interpolation]]. If $x=x_j$ for a node, then $f(x)-p(x)=0$. If $x\\in[a,b]$ is not a node, there is\n$$\\xi\\in\\bigl(\\min\\{x,x_0\\},\\max\\{x,x_n\\}\\bigr)$$\nsuch that\n$$f(x)-p(x)=\\frac{f^{(n+1)}(\\xi)}{(n+1)!_{\\mathbb R}}\\,\\omega_n(x),$$\nwhere $(n+1)!_{\\mathbb R}:=\\prod_{j=1}^{n+1}j$, with each natural factor read as its canonical real image under [[def-canonical-natural]].",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "def-chebyshev-arcsine-weighted-l2-pairing",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For continuous real functions $f,g$ on $[-1,1]$, define\n$$\n\\langle f,g\\rangle_C:=\\int_0^\\pi f(\\cos\\theta)g(\\cos\\theta)\\,d\\theta,\\qquad \\|f\\|_C^2:=\\langle f,f\\rangle_C.\n$$\nThis is the arcsine-weighted pairing on the compact Chebyshev interval: in the variable $x=\\cos\\theta$ its weight is $\\omega_C(x)=(1-x^2)^{-1/2}$ for $-1<x<1$. The angular integral is the endpoint convention, so no endpoint value of the singular density is required.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "def-higher-derivatives-and-smoothness",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval and $f:I\\to\\mathbb R$. Put $f^{(0)}:=f$. Recursively, wherever $f^{(j)}$ is differentiable, put $f^{(j+1)}:=(f^{(j)})'$, with derivatives at endpoints understood in the one-sided sense fixed by [[def-derivative]] and [[def-one-sided-limits]].\n\nFor $k\\in\\mathbb N$, the function is **$k$-times differentiable** on $I$ if $f^{(j)}$ exists on $I$ for every $j\\le k$. It is of class **$C^k$** on $I$ if these derivatives exist and every $f^{(j)}$, $0\\le j\\le k$, is continuous on $I$ ([[def-continuity-real]]). It is **smooth**, or $C^\\infty$, if it is $C^k$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nSince $0\\in\\mathbb N$ ([[def-natural-numbers]]), $C^0$ means continuity. The definitions also give $C^{k+1}\\subseteq C^k$. Existence of $f^{(k)}$ alone does not assert that $f^{(k)}$ is continuous.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-heine-borel-r",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a \\le b$. Then the closed bounded interval\n$[a,b]$ ([[def-interval]]) is compact ([[def-open-cover-r]]): every family of\nopen subsets of $\\mathbb{R}$ whose union contains $[a,b]$ has a finite subfamily\nwhose union already contains $[a,b]$.\n\nThe proof is by repeated bisection. Supposing some open cover admits no finite\nsubcover, one halves the interval, keeps a half that still admits none, and\niterates; the halves shrink to a point, which the cover does reach, and a single\nmember of the cover then swallows a whole late-stage half. **The halving rule is\ncanonical**, taking the left half whenever the left half works, so the recursion\nuses [[thm-recursion]] and no choice principle.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-extreme-value-r",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $f : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be continuous on $A$\n([[def-continuity-real]]) and let $K \\subseteq A$ be **nonempty** and compact\n([[def-open-cover-r]]). Then $\\sup f[K]$ and $\\inf f[K]$ exist and are\n**attained**: there are $p, q \\in K$ with\n\n$$f(q) \\;=\\; \\inf f[K] \\;\\le\\; f(x) \\;\\le\\; \\sup f[K] \\;=\\; f(p) \\qquad \\text{for every } x \\in K .$$\n\nEquivalently, the set $f[K]$ has a maximum and a minimum\n([[def-max-min]]), namely $\\max f[K] = f(p)$ and $\\min f[K] = f(q)$.\n\n**Nonemptiness of $K$ is a hypothesis, not an oversight.** For $K = \\varnothing$\nthe set $f[K]$ is empty, and neither a supremum nor a maximum of the empty set\nexists in this library ([[def-complete-ordered-field]] supplies suprema of\nnonempty sets bounded above only).\n\n**This theorem is stated twice in this library, on purpose.** Its metric-space\ntwin is [[thm-extreme-value-metric]], proved from the cover machinery of metric\nspaces; the proof below is $\\mathbb{R}$-native, running through Heine-Borel for\n$\\mathbb{R}$ and the order-completeness of $\\mathbb{R}$, and it uses no cover\nargument beyond the one already spent in\n[[thm-continuous-image-of-a-compact-set-r]]. That the two statements are the\nsame statement in two vocabularies is proved in\n[[lem-real-and-metric-notions-agree]], later on this page.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "thm-nonzero-real-polynomial-has-at-most-degree-many-distinct-roots",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "A nonzero real polynomial of degree $n$ has at most $n$ distinct real roots. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[def-real-polynomial-degree-leading-coefficient-and-monic]], [[lem-real-polynomial-factor-at-a-root]], [[thm-induction-principle]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "By the recurrence in [F2], $T_N$ has leading coefficient $2^{N-1}$ for every $N\\ge1$. Hence $q_N(x):=\\frac{2^{1-N}}{N}(x^2-1)T_N'(x)$ is monic of degree $N+1$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "For $1\\le j<N$, put $\\theta_j=j\\pi/N$. Then [F3] and [F4] give $T_N'(x_j)=N\\sin(j\\pi)/\\sin\\theta_j=0$. Also $q_N(1)=q_N(-1)=0$. Thus $q_N$ vanishes at all $N+1$ distinct nodes in [F1]. The node product $\\omega_N$ is also monic of degree $N+1$ by [F6]; their difference has degree at most $N$ and $N+1$ distinct roots, so [F10] gives $q_N=\\omega_N$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F6",
            "F10",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For $x=\\cos\\theta$ with $0<\\theta<\\pi$, the derivative formula in [F3] gives $|\\omega_N(x)|=2^{1-N}|\\sin\\theta\\,\\sin(N\\theta)|\\le2^{1-N}.$ The inequality uses [F5]. At the endpoints $\\omega_N=0$, so this bound holds on all of $[-1,1]$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "At nodes the interpolation error is zero; off the nodes apply [F7] and step 2.1. Since the maximum in [F9] is finite, this gives $\\|f-I_N^Lf\\|_\\infty\\le \\frac{2^{1-N}}{(N+1)!}\\max_{[-1,1]}|f^{(N+1)}|.$",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F7",
            "F9",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Apply the weighted-norm estimate [F8] to the uniform error in step 3.1. This proves the stated inequality.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F8",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
