    "thm-chebyshev-lobatto-interpolation-exponential-rate-for-analytic-data": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-cauchy-theorem-one-exceptional-point-on-a-star-shaped-domain",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb C$ be open and star-shaped, let $p\\in U$, and let $f:U\\to\\mathbb C$ be continuous and holomorphic on $U\\setminus\\{p\\}$. Then $f$ has a primitive on $U$, and for every closed rectifiable contour $\\gamma$ in $U$,\n\n$$\\int_\\gamma f(z)\\,dz=0.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-chebyshev-multiple-angle-identities",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every $n\\in\\mathbb N$ and real $\\theta$,\n$$T_n(\\cos\\theta)=\\cos(n\\theta),\\qquad U_n(\\cos\\theta)\\sin\\theta=\\sin((n+1)\\theta).$$ In particular, for $n\\ge1$ and $0\\le j\\le n$, $T_n(\\cos(j\\pi/n))=(-1)^j$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[def-chebyshev-polynomials-first-and-second-kind]], [[thm-sine-and-cosine-addition-formulas]], [[cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity]], [[thm-induction-principle]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$,\n$$\\sin(x+\\pi/2)=\\cos x,\\quad\\cos(x+\\pi/2)=-\\sin x,\\quad\\sin(x+\\pi)=-\\sin x,\\quad\\cos(x+\\pi)=-\\cos x.$$\nIn particular,\n$$\\sin(\\pi/2)=1,\\quad\\cos(\\pi/2)=0,\\quad\\sin\\pi=0,\\quad\\cos\\pi=-1.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-chebyshev-lobatto-mode-aliasing",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $N\\ge1$, let $x_j=\\cos(j\\pi/N)$, and let $I_N^L$ denote degree-at-most-$N$ interpolation on these nodes. For every integer $n\\ge0$, let $\\rho_N(n)$ be the unique integer $r\\in\\{0,\\ldots,N\\}$ congruent to either $n$ or $-n$ modulo $2N$. Then\n$$\nI_N^L T_n=T_{\\rho_N(n)}.\n$$\nIn particular, sampling a Chebyshev series on the Lobatto grid can fold every degree $n>N$ to a degree at most $N$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-chebyshev-polynomials-orthogonal-in-arcsine-weight",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Under the pairing of [[def-chebyshev-arcsine-weighted-l2-pairing]],\n$$\n\\langle T_m,T_n\\rangle_C=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\pi,&m=n=0,\\\\\n\\pi/2,&m=n\\ge1,\\\\\n0,&m\\ne n.\n\\end{cases}\n$$\nThe polynomials $T_0,T_1,\\ldots$ form a basis of the real polynomial space, and the pairing is positive definite on continuous real functions on $[-1,1]$. If a continuous real function $h$ satisfies $\\langle h,T_n\\rangle_C=0$ for every $n\\ge0$, then $h=0$ on $[-1,1]$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-chebyshev-arcsine-weighted-l2-pairing",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For continuous real functions $f,g$ on $[-1,1]$, define\n$$\n\\langle f,g\\rangle_C:=\\int_0^\\pi f(\\cos\\theta)g(\\cos\\theta)\\,d\\theta,\\qquad \\|f\\|_C^2:=\\langle f,f\\rangle_C.\n$$\nThis is the arcsine-weighted pairing on the compact Chebyshev interval: in the variable $x=\\cos\\theta$ its weight is $\\omega_C(x)=(1-x^2)^{-1/2}$ for $-1<x<1$. The angular integral is the endpoint convention, so no endpoint value of the singular density is required.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Sine is strictly increasing on each interval $[-\\pi/2+2m\\pi,\\pi/2+2m\\pi]$ and strictly decreasing on each interval $[\\pi/2+2m\\pi,3\\pi/2+2m\\pi]$. Cosine is strictly decreasing on $[2m\\pi,(2m+1)\\pi]$ and strictly increasing on $[(2m+1)\\pi,(2m+2)\\pi]$. Both functions have range $[-1,1]$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-geometric-series",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $r \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and let $r^k$ be the integer power ([[def-integer-power]]),\nso that $r^0 = 1$ for every $r$, including $r = 0$.\n\n1. If $|r| < 1$ then the series $\\sum r^k$ converges ([[def-series]]) and\n   $$\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty} r^{k} \\;=\\; \\frac{1}{1-r} .$$\n2. If $|r| \\ge 1$ then $\\sum r^k$ diverges.\n\nThe series starts at $k = 0$ and its first term is $r^0 = 1$; in particular\n$\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty} 2^{-k} = 2$, while the series starting at $k = 1$ sums to\n$1$. Which starting index is meant has to be said, and it is said here.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For $z=\\theta-it$ with $0\\le t\\le\\log\\rho$, $\\cos z=\\cos\\theta\\cosh t+i\\sin\\theta\\sinh t.$ For fixed $t>0$, its real and imaginary parts satisfy $x^2/\\cosh^2t+y^2/\\sinh^2t=1$, so the image is the Bernstein ellipse with parameter $e^t$; these ellipses are nested. Since $f$ is holomorphic on a neighborhood of $E_\\rho$, $g(z):=f(\\cos z)$ is holomorphic on an open strip containing the rectangle $-\\pi\\le\\Re z\\le\\pi$, $-\\log\\rho\\le\\Im z\\le0$. On its lower edge $|g(\\theta-i\\log\\rho)|\\le M$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For $n\\ge1$, set $H_n(z)=g(z)e^{-inz}$. It is holomorphic on that strip and $2\\pi$-periodic. Apply [F1] to the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $-\\pi,\\pi,\\pi-i\\log\\rho,-\\pi-i\\log\\rho$. The vertical integrals cancel by periodicity, so the top and lower horizontal integrals, both oriented from left to right, are equal. By [F2] and evenness, the coefficient in [F4] is $c_n=\\frac1\\pi\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}g(\\theta)e^{-in\\theta}\\,d\\theta.$ On the lower edge, $|e^{-in(\\theta-i\\log\\rho)}|=\\rho^{-n}$, so the shifted integral gives $|c_n|\\le2M\\rho^{-n}$. Also [F4] and $|f|\\le M$ give $|c_0|\\le M$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The coefficient bounds in step 2.1 make $\\sum_{n\\ge0}c_nT_n$ uniformly convergent, since [F2] and [F6] give $|T_n(x)|\\le1$. Uniform convergence permits termwise integration against each bounded $T_k$; orthogonality [F4] shows that this series and $f$ have the same Chebyshev coefficients. Their continuous difference therefore has every coefficient zero, so [F4] gives $f=\\sum_{n\\ge0}c_nT_n$ on $[-1,1]$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F6",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "By [F3], every mode $T_n$ with $n>N$ interpolates to a Chebyshev mode. Each such mode has sup norm at most one by [F2] and [F6]. Thus the series tail and its interpolant each have sup norm at most $\\sum_{n>N}|c_n|$, and $\\|f-I_N^Lf\\|_\\infty\\le2\\sum_{n>N}|c_n|\\le4M\\sum_{n>N}\\rho^{-n}=\\frac{4M}{\\rho-1}\\rho^{-N},$ where [F7] evaluates the geometric tail.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "Apply [F5] to the uniform error in step 4.1. This yields $\\|f-I_N^Lf\\|_C\\le4\\sqrt\\pi M(\\rho-1)^{-1}\\rho^{-N}$, as claimed.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "thm-chebyshev-lobatto-interpolation-exponential-rate-for-analytic-data has no empty-set or empty-interval branch beyond its explicit hypotheses."
