    "thm-composite-simpson-error-for-chebyshev-weighted-mass-matrix": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-chebyshev-arcsine-weighted-l2-pairing",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For continuous real functions $f,g$ on $[-1,1]$, define\n$$\n\\langle f,g\\rangle_C:=\\int_0^\\pi f(\\cos\\theta)g(\\cos\\theta)\\,d\\theta,\\qquad \\|f\\|_C^2:=\\langle f,f\\rangle_C.\n$$\nThis is the arcsine-weighted pairing on the compact Chebyshev interval: in the variable $x=\\cos\\theta$ its weight is $\\omega_C(x)=(1-x^2)^{-1/2}$ for $-1<x<1$. The angular integral is the endpoint convention, so no endpoint value of the singular density is required.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "alg-chebyshev-composite-simpson-weighted-mass-matrix",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Input a polynomial degree $N\\ge0$ and an even integer $M\\ge2$ specifying the number of uniform subintervals of $[0,\\pi]$. For $0\\le i,j\\le N$, form\n$$\ng_{ij}(\\theta)=T_i(\\cos\\theta)T_j(\\cos\\theta)\n$$\nand return\n$$\nM^{S}_{ij}:=Q_S(g_{ij};0,\\pi,M),\n$$\nwhere $Q_S$ is the composite Simpson procedure from [[alg-composite-simpson-rule]]. By the Chebyshev multiple-angle identity, $g_{ij}(\\theta)=\\cos(i\\theta)\\cos(j\\theta)$, so this is the composite-Simpson approximation of the corresponding arcsine-weighted mass entry. The procedure uses exact real arithmetic and asserts no quadrature or floating-point accuracy beyond a separately proved bound.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-composite-simpson-error-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a<b$, let $n\\ge2$ be an even integer, and put $h=(b-a)/n$. If\n$f\\in C^4([a,b])$, set $M_4=\\max_{x\\in[a,b]}|f^{(4)}(x)|$. Then the value\n$Q_S(f;a,b,n)$ of [[alg-composite-simpson-rule]] satisfies\n\n$$\\left|\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx-Q_S(f;a,b,n)\\right|\\le\\frac{(b-a)h^4}{180}M_4.$$\n\nThe integral is the Riemann integral. This is an exact-arithmetic quadrature\nbound; evaluation and summation roundoff are not included.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-chebyshev-multiple-angle-identities",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every $n\\in\\mathbb N$ and real $\\theta$,\n$$T_n(\\cos\\theta)=\\cos(n\\theta),\\qquad U_n(\\cos\\theta)\\sin\\theta=\\sin((n+1)\\theta).$$ In particular, for $n\\ge1$ and $0\\le j\\le n$, $T_n(\\cos(j\\pi/n))=(-1)^j$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[def-chebyshev-polynomials-first-and-second-kind]], [[thm-sine-and-cosine-addition-formulas]], [[cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity]], [[thm-induction-principle]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-sine-and-cosine-addition-formulas",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all real $x,y$,\n$$\\sin(x+y)=\\sin x\\cos y+\\cos x\\sin y,$$\n$$\\cos(x+y)=\\cos x\\cos y-\\sin x\\sin y.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The functions $\\sin$ and $\\cos$ are differentiable on $\\mathbb R$, with\n$$(\\sin x)'=\\cos x,\\qquad(\\cos x)'=-\\sin x.$$\nAlso $\\sin0=0$ and $\\cos0=1$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "By [F4], $g_{ij}(\\theta)=\\tfrac12\\{\\cos((i+j)\\theta)+\\cos((i-j)\\theta)\\}.$ This finite trigonometric expression is $C^4$ on $[0,\\pi]$. Its fourth derivative is $g_{ij}^{(4)}(\\theta)=\\tfrac12\\{(i+j)^4\\cos((i+j)\\theta)+(i-j)^4\\cos((i-j)\\theta)\\},$ by four applications of [F5]. Therefore $\\max_{[0,\\pi]}|g_{ij}^{(4)}|\\le\\tfrac12((i+j)^4+|i-j|^4)\\le(i+j)^4.$ This also covers $i=j=0$, when the fourth derivative is zero.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Apply [F3] to $g_{ij}$ and use [F1]–[F2] to identify the exact integral and the Simpson approximation. The derivative bound in step 1.1 gives the claimed entrywise error.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "thm-composite-simpson-error-for-chebyshev-weighted-mass-matrix has no empty-set or empty-interval branch beyond its explicit hypotheses."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement includes a zero mode/index, handled in step 1.1."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "thm-composite-simpson-error-for-chebyshev-weighted-mass-matrix has no separate one-size regime beyond the stated parameter range."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "thm-composite-simpson-error-for-chebyshev-weighted-mass-matrix has no additional degenerate interval or collapsed-grid branch beyond its stated hypotheses."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement and step 2.1 include the stated interval or grid endpoints."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement and steps 1.1 and 2.1 use explicit finite data or theorem-provided witnesses; the axiom_use field records the applicable choice branch."
        },
