    "thm-weighted-orthogonal-polynomial-galerkin-projection-is-well-defined": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-weighted-orthogonal-polynomial-galerkin-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mu$ be a finite positive Borel measure on a compact interval $[a,b]$ with infinite relative support. For each integer $N\\ge0$, the moment data $(a,b,m=N+1,r=2N+1,\\mu)$ satisfy the hypotheses of [[def-gauss-moment-data]]. Let $p_0,\\ldots,p_N$ be the monic orthogonal polynomials supplied by [[thm-monic-gauss-orthogonal-polynomials-exist-uniquely]], and write\n$$\nh_n=\\int_{[a,b]}p_n(x)^2\\,d\\mu(x)>0.\n$$\nThe finite-dimensional weighted Galerkin space is\n$$\nV_N(\\mu)=\\operatorname{span}\\{p_0,\\ldots,p_N\\}\\subset L^2(\\mu).\n$$\nHere $L^2(\\mu)$ means measurable functions with finite integral of the squared absolute value, modulo equality $\\mu$-almost everywhere.\n\nFor a coefficient sequence $(c_n)_{n\\ge0}$ with $\\sum_{n\\ge0}(1+n^2)^s|c_n|^2h_n<\\infty$, define its weighted spectral Sobolev norm by\n$$\n\\left\\|\\sum_{n\\ge0}c_np_n\\right\\|_{H^s_\\mu}^2\n=\\sum_{n\\ge0}(1+n^2)^s|c_n|^2h_n,\\qquad s\\ge0.\n$$\nThe associated orthogonal Galerkin projection is $\\Pi_N(\\sum_{n\\ge0}c_np_n)=\\sum_{n=0}^Nc_np_n$. This coefficient-defined scale is specific to the weight $\\mu$ and the selected orthogonal-polynomial family; no equivalence with a derivative-based Sobolev norm is asserted here.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-gauss-polynomial-moment-pairing-is-an-inner-product",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(a,b,m,r,\\mu)$ be moment data as in\n[[def-gauss-moment-data]]. For $0\\le k\\le m$ with $2k\\le r$, the pairing\n[[def-gauss-polynomial-moment-inner-product]] is a well-defined real inner\nproduct on $\\mathbb R^{k+1}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Each numerator is finite by [F3], and each denominator $h_j$ is finite and strictly positive by [F1]–[F2]. Thus every coefficient in the displayed finite sum is a defined real number.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Orthogonality of the $p_j$ gives, for each $i\\le N$, $\\int (u-\\Pi_Nu)p_i\\,d\\mu =\\int u p_i\\,d\\mu-\\sum_{j=0}^{N}\\frac{\\int u p_j\\,d\\mu}{h_j}\\int p_jp_i\\,d\\mu =0,$ because all terms with $j\\ne i$ vanish and the $j=i$ term equals $\\int u p_i\\,d\\mu$. Thus $\\Pi_Nu\\in V_N(\\mu)$ has the required residual orthogonality.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "If $v\\in V_N(\\mu)$ has the same residual property, then $w=v-\\Pi_Nu$ lies in $V_N(\\mu)$ and is orthogonal to every basis vector, hence to itself. Positive definiteness in [F2] gives $\\int w^2\\,d\\mu=0$, so $w=0$ in $L^2(\\mu)$ and $v=\\Pi_Nu$. This proves uniqueness.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "thm-weighted-orthogonal-polynomial-galerkin-projection-is-well-defined has no empty-set or empty-interval branch beyond its explicit hypotheses."
