    "thm-gaussian-orthogonal-polynomial-galerkin-mass-matrix-exact": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-gauss-rule-exact-through-degree-2m-minus-1-and-positive-weights",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(a,b,m,2m-1,\\mu)$ be moment data as in\n[[def-gauss-moment-data]], and let $Q_m$ and $w_i$ be the rule and weights of\n[[def-gauss-quadrature-rule]]. For every real polynomial $f$ of degree at most\n$2m-1$,\n$$ \\int_{[a,b]} f(x)\\,d\\mu(x)=Q_m(f)=\\sum_{i=1}^{m}w_if(x_i). $$\nMoreover, $w_i>0$ for every $i$ and $\\sum_iw_i=\\mu([a,b])$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-weighted-orthogonal-polynomial-galerkin-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mu$ be a finite positive Borel measure on a compact interval $[a,b]$ with infinite relative support. For each integer $N\\ge0$, the moment data $(a,b,m=N+1,r=2N+1,\\mu)$ satisfy the hypotheses of [[def-gauss-moment-data]]. Let $p_0,\\ldots,p_N$ be the monic orthogonal polynomials supplied by [[thm-monic-gauss-orthogonal-polynomials-exist-uniquely]], and write\n$$\nh_n=\\int_{[a,b]}p_n(x)^2\\,d\\mu(x)>0.\n$$\nThe finite-dimensional weighted Galerkin space is\n$$\nV_N(\\mu)=\\operatorname{span}\\{p_0,\\ldots,p_N\\}\\subset L^2(\\mu).\n$$\nHere $L^2(\\mu)$ means measurable functions with finite integral of the squared absolute value, modulo equality $\\mu$-almost everywhere.\n\nFor a coefficient sequence $(c_n)_{n\\ge0}$ with $\\sum_{n\\ge0}(1+n^2)^s|c_n|^2h_n<\\infty$, define its weighted spectral Sobolev norm by\n$$\n\\left\\|\\sum_{n\\ge0}c_np_n\\right\\|_{H^s_\\mu}^2\n=\\sum_{n\\ge0}(1+n^2)^s|c_n|^2h_n,\\qquad s\\ge0.\n$$\nThe associated orthogonal Galerkin projection is $\\Pi_N(\\sum_{n\\ge0}c_np_n)=\\sum_{n=0}^Nc_np_n$. This coefficient-defined scale is specific to the weight $\\mu$ and the selected orthogonal-polynomial family; no equivalence with a derivative-based Sobolev norm is asserted here.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "alg-gaussian-orthogonal-polynomial-galerkin-mass-matrix",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Input a degree $N\\ge0$, the monic orthogonal basis $p_0,\\ldots,p_N$ and the $(N+1)$-node Gaussian rule for the same positive measure $\\mu$ from [[def-gauss-quadrature-rule]]. With Gaussian nodes $\\xi_1,\\ldots,\\xi_{N+1}$ and weights $w_1,\\ldots,w_{N+1}$, output the matrix\n$$\nM_{ij}^{G}:=\\sum_{r=1}^{N+1}w_r p_i(\\xi_r)p_j(\\xi_r),\\qquad 0\\le i,j\\le N.\n$$\nThe inputs are admissible only when the moment data have order at least $2N+1$ and the rule nodes, weights, and basis all use the same measure. The algorithm performs the displayed finite exact-arithmetic sums; no floating-point conditioning claim is made.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The product $p_i p_j$ has degree $i+j\\le2N<2N+1=2(N+1)-1$. Thus [F1] applies with $m=N+1$ and gives $M_{ij}^{G}=\\sum_{r=1}^{N+1}w_rp_i(\\xi_r)p_j(\\xi_r)=\\int p_i(x)p_j(x)\\,d\\mu(x)$ by [F3].",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "If $i\\ne j$, the higher-degree polynomial is orthogonal to the lower-degree one by [F2], so the integral in step 1.1 is zero. If $i=j$, it is $h_i$ by [F2]. This is the displayed diagonal matrix identity.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
