    "lem-legendre-weighted-derivative-orthogonality-and-norm": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-legendre-polynomial-family-and-l2-pairing",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For each integer $n\\ge0$, define the Legendre polynomial\n$$\nP_n(x):=\\frac{1}{2^n n!}\\frac{d^n}{dx^n}(x^2-1)^n,\n$$\nwith $n!$ as in [[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]] and repeated derivatives as in [[def-higher-derivatives-and-smoothness]]. Define the unweighted pairing of real polynomials by\n$$\n\\langle p,q\\rangle_L:=\\int_{-1}^1p(x)q(x)\\,dx,\\qquad \\|p\\|_L^2:=\\langle p,p\\rangle_L.\n$$\nThe integral is the ordinary integral of a continuous function. The normalization and all polynomial identities needed for the orthogonal Galerkin scale are proved in [[lem-legendre-weighted-derivative-orthogonality-and-norm]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "5.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-integration-by-parts",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a < b$ be reals and let $u, v : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable at\nevery point of $[a,b]$ as functions on $[a,b]$ ([[def-derivative]]). Suppose\n$u'$ and $v'$ are integrable on $[a,b]$ ([[def-darboux-integral]]). Then $uv'$\nand $u'v$ are integrable and\n\n$$\\int_a^b u\\,v' \\;=\\; u(b)v(b) - u(a)v(a) \\;-\\; \\int_a^b u'\\,v .$$\n\n**The integrability of $u'$ and $v'$ is a hypothesis, not a formality.** Without\nit the two integrals in the display need not exist at all, and the identity is\nthen not false but ill-formed; that is\nthe false statement that deletes it on the companion\npage. The hypothesis is automatic when $u$ and $v$ are continuously\ndifferentiable, since a continuous function on $[a,b]$ is integrable\n([[thm-continuous-implies-integrable]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-derivative-of-a-power",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Powers are those of [[def-integer-power]], and $\\iota : \\mathbb{N} \\to\n\\mathbb{R}$ is the canonical natural of [[def-canonical-natural]], so that\n$\\iota(0) = 0$ and $\\iota(m+1) = \\iota(m) + 1$. Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\n1. The function $p_0 : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$, $p_0(x) = x^{0}$, is the\n   constant function $1$, and it is differentiable at every $c \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n   with $p_0'(c) = 0$ ([[def-derivative]]).\n2. For $n \\ge 1$ the function $p_n : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$,\n   $p_n(x) = x^{n}$, is differentiable at every $c \\in \\mathbb{R}$, and\n   $$p_n'(c) \\;=\\; \\iota(n)\\,c^{\\,n-1} .$$\n3. For $n \\ge 1$ put $A_0 := \\{\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R} : x \\ne 0 \\,\\}$. The function\n   $m_n : A_0 \\to \\mathbb{R}$, $m_n(x) = x^{-n}$, is differentiable at every\n   $c \\in A_0$ as a function on $A_0$, and\n   $$m_n'(c) \\;=\\; -\\,\\iota(n)\\,c^{\\,-n-1} .$$\n4. Let $a : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with $a_j = 0$ for $j \\ge n$, and let\n   $P : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be the polynomial function\n   $P(x) = \\sum_{j<n} a_j x^{j}$ ([[def-finite-sum]]). Then $P$ is\n   differentiable at every $c \\in \\mathbb{R}$, and, defining $e : \\mathbb{N}\n   \\to \\mathbb{R}$ by $e_0 := 0$ and $e_j := \\iota(j)\\,a_j\\,c^{\\,j-1}$ for\n   $j \\ge 1$,\n   $$P'(c) \\;=\\; \\sum_{j<n} e_j .$$\n\n**Claim 2 is stated for $n \\ge 1$ and not for $n \\ge 0$, and that is not\ntimidity.** At $n = 0$ its right-hand side reads $\\iota(0)\\,c^{-1}$, and\n$c^{-1}$ is not defined at $c = 0$ ([[def-integer-power]]), so the formula is\nnot a statement about the whole line. Claim 1 is what covers $n = 0$, and it\nsays the derivative is $0$ there, which is what the informal reading\n\"$0 \\cdot c^{-1}$\" is reaching for. The same shift is why the term $e_0$ of\nclaim 4 is defined to be $0$ outright rather than by the formula.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of $A$\n([[def-limit-point-r]]), let $f, g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable at $c$\n([[def-derivative]]) and let $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then:\n\n1. $f + g$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(f+g)'(c) = f'(c) + g'(c)$;\n2. $\\alpha f$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(\\alpha f)'(c) = \\alpha f'(c)$;\n3. $fg$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(fg)'(c) = f'(c)g(c) + f(c)g'(c)$;\n4. if $g(c) \\ne 0$ then, writing $A_0 := \\{\\, x \\in A : g(x) \\ne 0 \\,\\}$, the\n   point $c$ lies in $A_0$ and is a limit point of $A_0$, the quotient\n   $(f/g)|_{A_0} : A_0 \\to \\mathbb{R}$, $x \\mapsto f(x)/g(x)$, is differentiable\n   at $c$ as a function on $A_0$, and\n   $$\\bigl((f/g)|_{A_0}\\bigr)'(c) \\;=\\; \\frac{f'(c)\\,g(c) - f(c)\\,g'(c)}{g(c)^{2}} .$$\n\nEach claim asserts two things: that the derivative on the left exists, and that\nit has the stated value. Both are proved.\n\n**Why claim 4 is stated on $A_0$.** The function $f/g$ is not defined where $g$\nvanishes, and $g$ may vanish at points of $A$ far from $c$; restricting to $A_0$\nis forced. That the restriction still has $c$ as a limit point, so that a\nderivative there means anything at all, is not free either, and it is the last\nclaim of [[lem-sign-preservation-near-a-limit]] applied to $g$. The hypothesis\nis $g(c) \\ne 0$, not \"$g$ vanishes nowhere\".\n\n**Everything is proved through\n[[thm-caratheodory-characterisation]].** No difference quotient is estimated and\nno limit theorem beyond continuity is used, so no choice principle is spent.\nThe four identities are four algebraic rearrangements of an increment, each\nfollowed by a reading of [[thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Write $(x^2-1)^n=(x-1)^n(x+1)^n$. For every $j<n$, its $j$th derivative vanishes at both endpoints: by the product rule, every term differentiates each factor at most $j<n$ times and retains a positive power of the factor that vanishes at that endpoint. Thus $n$ integrations by parts in [F2] give, for every polynomial $q$ of degree less than $n$, $\\int_{-1}^1P_nq=\\frac{(-1)^n}{2^nn!}\\int_{-1}^1(x^2-1)^nq^{(n)}=0,$ because $q^{(n)}=0$ by [F3]. Hence $P_n$ is orthogonal to every lower-degree polynomial.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "given",
            "induction"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The leading term of $(x^2-1)^n$ is $x^{2n}$; differentiating it $n$ times gives $(2n)!/n!$ times $x^n$ by [F3]. All other terms have lower degree after differentiation. Therefore $P_n$ has degree $n$ and leading coefficient $\\kappa_n=(2n)!/(2^n(n!)^2)>0$. The distinct degrees imply the family $P_0,\\ldots,P_N$ is linearly independent and spans every polynomial of degree at most $N$, by successive elimination of leading terms.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Since $P_n-\\kappa_nx^n$ has degree less than $n$, step 1.1 gives $\\int_{-1}^1P_n^2=\\kappa_n\\int_{-1}^1P_nx^n.$ Apply [F2] $n$ times to the Rodrigues formula against $x^n$. The endpoint terms vanish as in step 1.1 and $(x^n)^{(n)}=n!$, so $\\int_{-1}^1P_nx^n=\\frac1{2^n}\\int_{-1}^1(1-x^2)^n\\,dx.$",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Put $J_n=\\int_{-1}^1(1-x^2)^n dx$. Then $J_0=2$. For $n\\ge1$, the endpoint values of $x(1-x^2)^n$ are zero, so integrating its derivative gives $0=J_n-2n(J_{n-1}-J_n),\\qquad J_n=\\frac{2n}{2n+1}J_{n-1}.$ Induction yields $J_n=2^{2n+1}(n!)^2/(2n+1)!$. Substituting this and $\\kappa_n$ from step 2.1 into step 3.1 gives $\\int P_n^2=2/(2n+1)$. Together with step 1.1 this proves the first orthogonality formula, including $n=0$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "induction",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "When $n=0$, $P_0=1$ and $Q_0=-((1-x^2)P_0')'=0$, so $Q_0=0\\cdot P_0$. Now take $n\\ge1$ and define $Q_n=-((1-x^2)P_n')'$. Its leading term is $n(n+1)\\kappa_nx^n$, so $Q_n-n(n+1)P_n$ has degree less than $n$. For any polynomial $q$ of degree less than $n$, two integrations by parts give $\\int_{-1}^1Q_nq=\\int_{-1}^1(1-x^2)P_n'q'=-\\int_{-1}^1P_n((1-x^2)q')'=0,$ where both boundary terms vanish because of the factor $1-x^2$, and the last polynomial paired with $P_n$ has degree less than $n$, so step 1.1 applies. Therefore $Q_n-n(n+1)P_n$ is a lower-degree polynomial orthogonal to every lower-degree polynomial. Expand it in the basis from step 2.1; the first formula and the positive norms in step 4.1 force every coefficient to be zero. Thus $Q_n=n(n+1)P_n$.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "claim": "One integration by parts and step 5.1 give $\\int_{-1}^1(1-x^2)P_n'P_m'=\\int_{-1}^1Q_nP_m=n(n+1)\\int_{-1}^1P_nP_m.$ The first formula, already proved in step 4.1 and step 1.1, yields the displayed weighted derivative identity for every $m,n\\ge0$.",
          "step": "6.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "step 5.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "lem-legendre-weighted-derivative-orthogonality-and-norm has no empty-set or empty-interval branch beyond its explicit hypotheses."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement includes a zero mode/index, handled in step 1.1."
        },
        {
