    "thm-legendre-weighted-projection-error-rate": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-legendre-weighted-sobolev-scale",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $s\\ge0$, define $H_L^s$ as the completion of finite Legendre sums $p=\\sum_{n=0}^Mc_nP_n$ in the coefficient norm\n$$\n\\|p\\|_{H_L^s}^2=\\sum_{n=0}^M(1+n(n+1))^s\\frac{2}{2n+1}|c_n|^2.\n$$\nThe coefficients are unique because the polynomials have distinct degrees by [[lem-legendre-weighted-derivative-orthogonality-and-norm]]. At $s=0$ this norm is exactly the unweighted $L^2([-1,1])$ norm on finite sums, by the same lemma; its completion is denoted $L_L^2$. Define the degree-$N$ orthogonal projection on finite sums by\n$$\n\\Pi_Nu=\\sum_{n=0}^Nc_nP_n.\n$$\nIt extends to the coefficient completion by truncation. This explicitly coefficient-defined scale uses the Legendre weight $1$ on $[-1,1]$; no equivalence with an unweighted derivative-based Sobolev norm is asserted here.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-legendre-weighted-derivative-orthogonality-and-norm",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $m,n\\ge0$, the polynomials of [[def-legendre-polynomial-family-and-l2-pairing]] satisfy\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^1P_n(x)P_m(x)\\,dx=\\frac{2}{2n+1}\\,\\delta_{mn},\n\\qquad\n\\int_{-1}^1(1-x^2)P_n'(x)P_m'(x)\\,dx=\\frac{2n(n+1)}{2n+1}\\,\\delta_{mn},\n$$\nwhere $\\delta_{mn}=1$ if $m=n$ and $0$ otherwise. Each $P_n$ has degree $n$ and nonzero leading coefficient, so $P_0,\\ldots,P_N$ form a basis of polynomials of degree at most $N$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For a finite Legendre sum, [F1] gives $u-\\Pi_Nu=\\sum_{n>N}c_nP_n$. Orthogonality [F2] therefore gives $\\|u-\\Pi_Nu\\|_{L_L^2}^2=\\sum_{n>N}\\frac{2}{2n+1}|c_n|^2.$ For every $n>N$, $1+n(n+1)\\ge1+(N+1)(N+2)$; multiplying each term by the inverse weight gives the stated squared bound for finite sums.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Finite sums are dense in the completion defining $H_L^s$. The truncation $\\Pi_N$ is contractive in both the coefficient norm and the $s=0$ norm, so the finite-sum inequality passes to the limit along any Cauchy sequence of finite sums. Taking square roots proves the claimed estimate for every $u\\in H_L^s$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "thm-legendre-weighted-projection-error-rate has no empty-set or empty-interval branch beyond its explicit hypotheses."
