    "ex-periodic-fourier-collocation-exactness-and-nyquist-mode": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-periodic-fourier-collocation-grid",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $N\\ge2$ be an integer, let $x_j=2\\pi j/N$ for $j=0,\\ldots,N-1$, and identify $x_N$ with $x_0$ periodically. The real trigonometric interpolation space is\n$$\n\\mathcal T_N=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\operatorname{span}_{\\mathbb R}\\{1,\\cos(kx),\\sin(kx):1\\le k\\le (N-1)/2\\},&N\\text{ odd},\\\\\n\\operatorname{span}_{\\mathbb R}\\{1,\\cos(kx),\\sin(kx):1\\le k<N/2\\}\\oplus\\operatorname{span}_{\\mathbb R}\\{\\cos(Nx/2)\\},&N\\text{ even}.\n\\end{cases}\n$$\nFor real nodal data $U=(U_0,\\ldots,U_{N-1})$, define its periodic Fourier interpolant by\n$$\nI_NU(x)=\\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}U_jL_j(x),\n$$\nwhere, for $N=2K+1$,\n$$\nL_j(x)=\\frac1N\\left(1+2\\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\cos(k(x-x_j))\\right),\n$$\nand, for $N=2K$,\n$$\nL_j(x)=\\frac1N\\left(1+2\\sum_{k=1}^{K-1}\\cos(k(x-x_j))+\\cos(K(x-x_j))\\right).\n$$\nThus the even-grid Nyquist component is the real cosine $\\cos(Nx/2)$ only. Its sampled derivative is defined by differentiating this interpolant; the resulting Nyquist derivative values are zero at every grid point.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$,\n$$\\sin(x+\\pi/2)=\\cos x,\\quad\\cos(x+\\pi/2)=-\\sin x,\\quad\\sin(x+\\pi)=-\\sin x,\\quad\\cos(x+\\pi)=-\\cos x.$$\nIn particular,\n$$\\sin(\\pi/2)=1,\\quad\\cos(\\pi/2)=0,\\quad\\sin\\pi=0,\\quad\\cos\\pi=-1.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The functions $\\sin$ and $\\cos$ are differentiable on $\\mathbb R$, with\n$$(\\sin x)'=\\cos x,\\qquad(\\cos x)'=-\\sin x.$$\nAlso $\\sin0=0$ and $\\cos0=1$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-periodic-fourier-collocation-differentiation-is-exact",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $N\\ge2$, let $U=(U_j)_{j=0}^{N-1}\\in\\mathbb R^N$, and let $p=I_NU\\in\\mathcal T_N$ be the interpolant defined in [[def-periodic-fourier-collocation-grid]]. The matrix [[alg-periodic-fourier-collocation-differentiation-matrix]] satisfies\n$$\n(D_NU)_i=p'(x_i),\\qquad 0\\le i<N.\n$$\nFor even $N$, this statement includes the Nyquist cosine mode, whose derivative vanishes at the grid nodes.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Substitution of $x_0=0,x_1=\\pi/2,x_2=\\pi,x_3=3\\pi/2$ into $p$ using [F2] gives respectively $-1,8,-5,2$. The frequencies $0,1,2$ lie in [F1]'s interpolation space, so $p$ is the interpolant of these samples.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "By [F3], $p'(x)=-2\\sin x+3\\cos x+8\\sin(2x)$. At the four nodes, the final term is zero and the first two terms give $3,-2,-3,2$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Exactness [F4] therefore gives $D_4U=(3,-2,-3,2)$, including the vanishing nodal derivative of the Nyquist mode.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "ex-periodic-fourier-collocation-exactness-and-nyquist-mode has no empty-set or empty-interval branch beyond its explicit hypotheses."
