    "ex-chebyshev-lobatto-endpoint-clustering": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-chebyshev-gauss-lobatto-collocation-grid",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $N\\ge1$ be an integer and put $x_j=\\cos(j\\pi/N)$ for $j=0,\\ldots,N$. The ordering is decreasing: $x_0=1$ and $x_N=-1$. These are the Chebyshev–Gauss–Lobatto nodes; [[thm-chebyshev-multiple-angle-identities]] gives $T_N(x_j)=(-1)^j$. They are distinct because cosine is strictly decreasing on $[0,\\pi]$ by [[thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges]].\n\nFor interpolation using [[def-cardinal-polynomial-interpolation]], use the increasing reindexing $z_k=x_{N-k}$, $k=0,\\ldots,N$. For descending-grid data $U_j$, the corresponding increasing data are $Y_k=U_{N-k}$.\n\nFor nodal data $U_0,\\ldots,U_N$, define $I_N^LU$ to be the cardinal interpolant of $(Y_k)_{k=0}^N$ at $(z_k)_{k=0}^N$. For a function $f$ defined on the interval, write $I_N^Lf$ for the interpolant of its nodal values. The increasing-node interpolation theorem [[thm-cardinal-polynomial-interpolant-exists-uniquely]] makes this polynomial unique.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-sine-and-cosine-subtraction-formulas",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all real $u,v$,\n$$\\sin(u-v)=\\sin u\\cos v-\\cos u\\sin v,\\qquad \\cos(u-v)=\\cos u\\cos v+\\sin u\\sin v.$$ The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[thm-sine-and-cosine-addition-formulas]], [[cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-sine-and-cosine-addition-formulas",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all real $x,y$,\n$$\\sin(x+y)=\\sin x\\cos y+\\cos x\\sin y,$$\n$$\\cos(x+y)=\\cos x\\cos y-\\sin x\\sin y.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-sine-chord-and-lipschitz-bounds",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $0\\leq u\\leq\\pi/2$,\n$$\\sin u\\geq \\frac{2u}{\\pi}.$$\nFor all real $u,v$,\n$$|\\sin u-\\sin v|\\leq |u-v|.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Sine is strictly increasing on each interval $[-\\pi/2+2m\\pi,\\pi/2+2m\\pi]$ and strictly decreasing on each interval $[\\pi/2+2m\\pi,3\\pi/2+2m\\pi]$. Cosine is strictly decreasing on $[2m\\pi,(2m+1)\\pi]$ and strictly increasing on $[(2m+1)\\pi,(2m+2)\\pi]$. Both functions have range $[-1,1]$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$,\n$$\\sin(x+\\pi/2)=\\cos x,\\quad\\cos(x+\\pi/2)=-\\sin x,\\quad\\sin(x+\\pi)=-\\sin x,\\quad\\cos(x+\\pi)=-\\cos x.$$\nIn particular,\n$$\\sin(\\pi/2)=1,\\quad\\cos(\\pi/2)=0,\\quad\\sin\\pi=0,\\quad\\cos\\pi=-1.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The subtraction and addition identities [F2] give $x_j-x_{j+1}=2\\sin\\left(\\frac{(2j+1)\\pi}{2N}\\right)\\sin\\left(\\frac\\pi{2N}\\right),\\qquad x_0-x_1=2\\sin^2\\left(\\frac\\pi{2N}\\right).$ By [F3], $\\sin(\\pi/(2N))\\le\\pi/(2N)$, hence $x_0-x_1\\le\\pi^2/(2N^2)$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "If $N$ is odd, choose $j=(N-1)/2$; then $(2j+1)\\pi/(2N)=\\pi/2$ and [F4] gives sine equal to $1$. If $N$ is even, choose $j=N/2-1$; then this angle is $\\pi/2-\\pi/(2N)\\ge\\pi/4$, so sine monotonicity [F4] and the chord bound [F3] give sine at least $\\sin(\\pi/4)\\ge1/2$. In both cases the first sine factor in step 1.1 is at least $1/2$; [F3] gives $\\sin(\\pi/(2N))\\ge1/N$. Thus the corresponding central gap is at least $1/N$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The ratio of the endpoint gap to this central gap is at most $\\pi^2/(2N)$, which tends to zero as $N\\to\\infty$. This proves the stated endpoint clustering and nonuniformity.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
