    "ex-analytic-and-nonsmooth-chebyshev-coefficient-decay": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-chebyshev-polynomials-first-and-second-kind",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal P_{\\mathbb R}$ be the set of formal real polynomials of\n[[def-real-polynomial-degree-leading-coefficient-and-monic]]. Associate to every\n$P\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathbb R}$ an eventually zero coefficient sequence $(p_k)$:\nuse its displayed finite coefficient list and extend it by zeros when $P\\ne0$,\nand put $p_k=0$ for every $k$ when $P=0$. Do the same for $Q$, with coefficients\n$(q_k)$. Define $P+Q$ coefficientwise and define $PQ$ by the convolution\ncoefficients\n$$ (P+Q)_k=p_k+q_k,\\qquad (PQ)_k=\\sum_{j=0}^{k}p_jq_{k-j}. $$\nusing the real finite sum of [[def-finite-sum]]. In either construction, discard\ntrailing zero coefficients; if every coefficient is zero, the result is the zero\npolynomial. Scalar multiplication and subtraction are the corresponding\ncoefficientwise operations. Thus these formulas define operations on the formal\ncoefficient-list objects, independently of evaluation.\n\nWrite $1=(1)$ and $X=(0,1)$, and define\n$$ \\Phi(P,Q):=(Q,2XQ-P)\\qquad(P,Q\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathbb R}). $$\nApply [[thm-recursion]] to the set\n$\\mathcal P_{\\mathbb R}\\times\\mathcal P_{\\mathbb R}$, first with initial value\n$(1,X)$ and then with initial value $(1,2X)$, always using the function $\\Phi$.\nThis gives unique pair sequences $H^T,H^U$. Define $T_n$ and $U_n$ to be the\nfirst coordinates of $H^T_n$ and $H^U_n$, respectively. Since the first\ncoordinate of $\\Phi(P,Q)$ is $Q$, the second coordinate of $H^T_n$ is\n$T_{n+1}$ and the second coordinate of $H^U_n$ is $U_{n+1}$. Consequently\n$$ T_0=1,\\quad T_1=X,\\quad T_{n+2}=2XT_{n+1}-T_n, $$\n$$ U_0=1,\\quad U_1=2X,\\quad U_{n+2}=2XU_{n+1}-U_n $$\nfor every $n\\in\\mathbb N$. These unique sequences are the **Chebyshev\npolynomials of the first and second kinds**.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-chebyshev-multiple-angle-identities",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every $n\\in\\mathbb N$ and real $\\theta$,\n$$T_n(\\cos\\theta)=\\cos(n\\theta),\\qquad U_n(\\cos\\theta)\\sin\\theta=\\sin((n+1)\\theta).$$ In particular, for $n\\ge1$ and $0\\le j\\le n$, $T_n(\\cos(j\\pi/n))=(-1)^j$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[def-chebyshev-polynomials-first-and-second-kind]], [[thm-sine-and-cosine-addition-formulas]], [[cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity]], [[thm-induction-principle]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-chebyshev-arcsine-weighted-l2-pairing",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For continuous real functions $f,g$ on $[-1,1]$, define\n$$\n\\langle f,g\\rangle_C:=\\int_0^\\pi f(\\cos\\theta)g(\\cos\\theta)\\,d\\theta,\\qquad \\|f\\|_C^2:=\\langle f,f\\rangle_C.\n$$\nThis is the arcsine-weighted pairing on the compact Chebyshev interval: in the variable $x=\\cos\\theta$ its weight is $\\omega_C(x)=(1-x^2)^{-1/2}$ for $-1<x<1$. The angular integral is the endpoint convention, so no endpoint value of the singular density is required.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-chebyshev-polynomials-orthogonal-in-arcsine-weight",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Under the pairing of [[def-chebyshev-arcsine-weighted-l2-pairing]],\n$$\n\\langle T_m,T_n\\rangle_C=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\pi,&m=n=0,\\\\\n\\pi/2,&m=n\\ge1,\\\\\n0,&m\\ne n.\n\\end{cases}\n$$\nThe polynomials $T_0,T_1,\\ldots$ form a basis of the real polynomial space, and the pairing is positive definite on continuous real functions on $[-1,1]$. If a continuous real function $h$ satisfies $\\langle h,T_n\\rangle_C=0$ for every $n\\ge0$, then $h=0$ on $[-1,1]$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "ex-complex-geometric-power-series",
          "source_section": "Example",
          "quote": "For $|z|<1$, the complex geometric series $\\sum_{n\\ge0}z^n$ has radius $1$ and sum $1/(1-z)$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[thm-cauchy-hadamard-for-complex-power-series]], [[thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field]], [[thm-geometric-series]], [[lem-geometric-sequence-null]], [[thm-absolute-convergence-of-complex-series]], [[lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws]], [[thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-complex-differentiability-holomorphic-and-entire",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb C$ be open, let $a\\in U$, and let $f:U\\to\\mathbb C$. The function $f$ is **complex differentiable at $a$** if the limit\n\n$$ \\lim_{\\substack{h\\to0\\\\ h\\ne0,\\ a+h\\in U}}\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h} $$\n\nexists in the metric of [[def-complex-metric-convergence-and-continuity]]. Its value is the **complex derivative** of $f$ at $a$, denoted $f'(a)$. The increments are nonzero and remain in the domain; because $U$ is open, all sufficiently small increments are allowed.\n\nThe function $f$ is **holomorphic on $U$** when it is complex differentiable at every point of $U$. A function holomorphic on all of $\\mathbb C$ is **entire**. The word analytic is reserved for the local power-series notion.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "$\\mathbb C=\\mathbb R[x]/(x^2+1)$ is a field containing the embedded copy of\n$\\mathbb R$. Every complex number has a unique form $a+bi$ with $a,b\\in\\mathbb R$,\nand\n$$ (a+bi)+(u+vi)=(a+u)+(b+v)i, $$\n$$ (a+bi)(u+vi)=(au-bv)+(av+bu)i. $$\nIf $a+bi\\ne0$, then\n$$ (a+bi)^{-1}=\\frac{a-bi}{a^2+b^2}. $$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-sine-and-cosine-addition-formulas",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all real $x,y$,\n$$\\sin(x+y)=\\sin x\\cos y+\\cos x\\sin y,$$\n$$\\cos(x+y)=\\cos x\\cos y-\\sin x\\sin y.$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The functions $\\sin$ and $\\cos$ are differentiable on $\\mathbb R$, with\n$$(\\sin x)'=\\cos x,\\qquad(\\cos x)'=-\\sin x.$$\nAlso $\\sin0=0$ and $\\cos0=1$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-ftc-second-part",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a < b$ be reals, let $G : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable at every\npoint of $[a,b]$ as a function on $[a,b]$ ([[def-derivative]]; at $a$ and $b$\nthis is the one-sided derivative), let $f := G'$, and suppose $f$ is integrable\non $[a,b]$ ([[def-darboux-integral]]). Then\n\n$$\\int_a^b f \\;=\\; G(b) - G(a) .$$\n\n**Both hypotheses are needed and neither is removable.** A function may be\ndifferentiable everywhere with $G'$ not integrable — then the left-hand side does\nnot exist (an everywhere differentiable function with unbounded derivative) — and an\nintegrable $f$ need not be the derivative of anything\n(the sign function); both witnesses are on the\ncompanion page.\n\n**No continuity of $f$ is assumed**, which is what makes this the working form:\nthe theorem evaluates $\\int_a^b f$ for every integrable derivative, not only for\ncontinuous integrands.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$,\n$$\\sin(x+\\pi/2)=\\cos x,\\quad\\cos(x+\\pi/2)=-\\sin x,\\quad\\sin(x+\\pi)=-\\sin x,\\quad\\cos(x+\\pi)=-\\cos x.$$\nIn particular,\n$$\\sin(\\pi/2)=1,\\quad\\cos(\\pi/2)=0,\\quad\\sin\\pi=0,\\quad\\cos\\pi=-1.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Sine is strictly increasing on each interval $[-\\pi/2+2m\\pi,\\pi/2+2m\\pi]$ and strictly decreasing on each interval $[\\pi/2+2m\\pi,3\\pi/2+2m\\pi]$. Cosine is strictly decreasing on $[2m\\pi,(2m+1)\\pi]$ and strictly increasing on $[(2m+1)\\pi,(2m+2)\\pi]$. Both functions have range $[-1,1]$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For real $x,y$, $\\exp(x+iy)=e^x(\\cos y+i\\sin y)$ and $|\\exp(x+iy)|=e^x$. In particular $e^{i\\pi}+1=0$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[thm-complex-exponential-addition-and-real-extension]], [[thm-eulers-formula]], [[lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws]], [[thm-pythagorean-and-parity-identities-for-all-six-trigonometric-functions]], [[cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity]], [[thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas]], [[def-pi-via-first-positive-cosine-zero]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-sine-and-cosine-subtraction-formulas",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all real $u,v$,\n$$\\sin(u-v)=\\sin u\\cos v-\\cos u\\sin v,\\qquad \\cos(u-v)=\\cos u\\cos v+\\sin u\\sin v.$$ The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[thm-sine-and-cosine-addition-formulas]], [[cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$,\n$$\\sin(-x)=-\\sin x,\\qquad\\cos(-x)=\\cos x,\\qquad\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x=1.$$\nConsequently $|\\sin x|\\le1$ and $|\\cos x|\\le1$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "def-derivative",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Throughout, $\\mathbb{R}$ is the complete ordered field\n([[def-complete-ordered-field]]), neighbourhoods are those of\n[[def-neighbourhood-r]] and limit points those of [[def-limit-point-r]].\n\nLet $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $f : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and let $c \\in A$ be a\n**limit point of $A$**. The **difference quotient of $f$ at $c$** is the function\n\n$$q_{f,c} : A \\setminus \\{c\\} \\to \\mathbb{R}, \\qquad q_{f,c}(x) := \\frac{f(x) - f(c)}{x - c} .$$\n\nThe division is legitimate at every point of the domain, since $x \\ne c$ gives\n$x - c \\ne 0$.\n\n**The point $c$ is a limit point of $A \\setminus \\{c\\}$, not merely of $A$.**\nFor every real $\\varepsilon > 0$ the punctured neighbourhood\n$N^{*}_{\\varepsilon}(c)$ omits $c$, so\n\n$$N^{*}_{\\varepsilon}(c) \\cap A \\;=\\; N^{*}_{\\varepsilon}(c) \\cap (A \\setminus \\{c\\}) ,$$\n\nand the left-hand side is nonempty because $c$ is a limit point of $A$. So\n$q_{f,c}$ is a function on a set having $c$ as a limit point, and\n$\\lim_{x \\to c} q_{f,c}(x)$ is a notion that [[def-function-limit]] defines.\n\n$f$ is **differentiable at $c$** when that limit exists, and then the\n**derivative of $f$ at $c$** is\n\n$$f'(c) \\;:=\\; \\lim_{x \\to c} q_{f,c}(x) \\;=\\; \\lim_{x \\to c} \\frac{f(x) - f(c)}{x - c} .$$\n\n**Two obligations are carried by that notation, and both are discharged here.**\n\n1. **Uniqueness.** Writing $f'(c)$ treats the right-hand side as a name for a\n   single real number. That is legitimate: $c$ is a limit point of the domain\n   $A \\setminus \\{c\\}$ of $q_{f,c}$, so at most one real can satisfy the\n   $\\varepsilon$-$\\delta$ condition, by [[lem-function-limit-unique]] applied to\n   $q_{f,c}$. Two reals both meeting the condition are therefore equal, and the\n   symbol denotes.\n2. **Meaningfulness.** The hypothesis that $c$ is a limit point of $A$ is not\n   decoration. At an **isolated** point of $A$ the punctured condition\n   $0 < |x - c| < \\delta$ is met by no point of the domain at all, so the\n   $\\varepsilon$-$\\delta$ formula is satisfied vacuously by *every* real at\n   once; this is why [[def-function-limit]] leaves the limit undefined there,\n   and it is why this library defines $f'(c)$ only at a limit point of $A$. At\n   an isolated point of its domain a function is neither differentiable nor\n   non-differentiable here: the question is not posed.\n\n**The limit sees only $A \\setminus \\{c\\}$, so how the difference quotient is\nextended to $c$ is irrelevant.** Let $Q : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ agree with\n$q_{f,c}$ at every point of $A \\setminus \\{c\\}$, and let $L \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\nThen $\\lim_{x \\to c} Q(x) = L$ if and only if $\\lim_{x \\to c} q_{f,c}(x) = L$.\nBoth conditions read: for every real $\\varepsilon > 0$ there is a real\n$\\delta > 0$ such that every point $x$ of the relevant domain with\n$0 < |x - c| < \\delta$ satisfies $|{\\cdot} - L| < \\varepsilon$\n([[def-function-limit]]). The clause $0 < |x - c|$ removes $x = c$ from both\nquantifiers, so in both cases the points quantified over are exactly the\n$x \\in A \\setminus \\{c\\}$ with $0 < |x - c| < \\delta$, at which $Q$ and\n$q_{f,c}$ take the same value. The two conditions are the same condition.\n\n**Differentiability on a set.** For $S \\subseteq A$, $f$ is **differentiable on\n$S$** when it is differentiable at every $c \\in S$; implicit in that phrase is\nthat every point of $S$ is a limit point of $A$. $f$ is **differentiable** when\nit is differentiable on the whole of $A$.\n\n**Restriction of the domain.** Let $B \\subseteq A$, let $c \\in B$ and suppose\n$c$ is a limit point of $B$. If $f$ is differentiable at $c$, then so is the\nrestriction $f|_B : B \\to \\mathbb{R}$, and\n\n$$(f|_B)'(c) \\;=\\; f'(c) .$$\n\nIndeed $B \\setminus \\{c\\} \\subseteq A \\setminus \\{c\\}$; the displayed identity\nof punctured neighbourhoods above, applied to $B$, shows that $c$ is a limit\npoint of $B \\setminus \\{c\\}$; the difference quotient $q_{f|_B, c}$ is the\nrestriction of $q_{f,c}$ to $B \\setminus \\{c\\}$, since $f|_B(c) = f(c)$; and\nclaim 2 of [[lem-limit-is-local]] carries the limit to that restriction.\n\n**Every point of a nondegenerate interval is a limit point of it.** Let\n$J \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ be order-convex ([[def-interval]]) with at least two\nelements and let $p \\in J$. Choose $q \\in J$ with $q \\ne p$, and let a real\n$\\varepsilon > 0$ be given. If $p < q$, put $y := p + \\tfrac{1}{2}\\min\\{\\varepsilon,\\ q - p\\}$;\nthen $p < y$, and $y - p \\le \\tfrac{1}{2}(q-p) < q - p$, so $p < y < q$ and\norder-convexity gives $y \\in J$, while $0 < |y - p| < \\varepsilon$. If $q < p$,\nthe point $y := p - \\tfrac{1}{2}\\min\\{\\varepsilon,\\ p - q\\}$ serves in the same\nway. So $N^{*}_{\\varepsilon}(p) \\cap J \\ne \\varnothing$ for every real\n$\\varepsilon > 0$, that is, $p$ is a limit point of $J$\n([[def-limit-point-r]]).\n\nConsequently, for $f$ defined on a nondegenerate interval $I$, the symbol\n$f'(c)$ is meaningful at **every** $c \\in I$, endpoints included. At an endpoint\nthe difference quotient is taken over the points of $I$ lying on the one side\nthat is available, so what other texts call a one-sided derivative is, here,\nsimply the derivative of $f$ on $I$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The set $\\Omega=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C:|z|<3/2\\}$ is an open neighborhood of $[-1,1]$ and omits $2$. For $z,z_0\\in\\Omega$ with $z\\ne z_0$, $\\frac{f_a(z)-f_a(z_0)}{z-z_0}=\\frac1{(2-z)(2-z_0)}\\longrightarrow\\frac1{(2-z_0)^2}\\quad(z\\to z_0).$ Thus [F4] makes $f_a$ holomorphic on a neighborhood of the interval.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F4",
            "F11",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "The function $f_s(x)=|x|$ is not differentiable at $0$: for $h>0$ its difference quotient is $1$, while for $h<0$ it is $-1$, so the two-sided limit in [F11] does not exist. By [F7], $|\\cos\\theta|=\\cos\\theta$ on $[0,\\pi/2]$ and $|\\cos\\theta|=-\\cos\\theta$ on $[\\pi/2,\\pi]$. The subtraction identity [F9] gives $\\cos(n(\\pi-\\theta))=(-1)^n\\cos(n\\theta)$, so reflection makes each odd coefficient integral its own negative. Therefore $a_0(f_s)=\\frac1\\pi\\int_0^\\pi|\\cos\\theta|\\,d\\theta=\\frac2\\pi.$ For odd $n$, that reflection makes $a_n(f_s)=0$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F9",
            "F11",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Put $q=2-\\sqrt3$. Then $0<q<1$, $q^{-1}=2+\\sqrt3$, $q+q^{-1}=4$, and $1-q^2=2\\sqrt3q$. By [F8], $z=qe^{i\\theta}=q(\\cos\\theta+i\\sin\\theta)$ has modulus $q<1$, so [F3] gives $\\sum_{n\\ge1}z^n=z/(1-z)$. Multiplying numerator and denominator by $1-q\\cos\\theta+iq\\sin\\theta$ and using [F10] yields $1+2\\sum_{n\\ge1}q^n\\cos(n\\theta)=1+2\\operatorname{Re}\\frac{z}{1-z}=\\frac{1-q^2}{1-2q\\cos\\theta+q^2}=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2-\\cos\\theta}.$ The real-part identity follows by direct expansion: $\\operatorname{Re}(z/(1-z))=(q\\cos\\theta-q^2)/(1-2q\\cos\\theta+q^2)$; the denominator is nonzero because $|z|<1$. Also $1-2q\\cos\\theta+q^2=2q(2-\\cos\\theta)$. The tail is bounded uniformly by $2\\sum_{n>m}q^n$, which tends to zero by [F3]. Dividing the displayed identity by $\\sqrt3$ gives $f_a(\\cos\\theta)=\\frac1{\\sqrt3}(1+2\\sum_{n\\ge1}q^n\\cos(n\\theta))$. By [F1]–[F2], this is its Chebyshev expansion and yields the stated coefficients.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F8",
            "F10",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For $m\\ge1$, symmetry about $\\pi/2$ and [F5] give $\\int_0^\\pi|\\cos\\theta|\\cos(2m\\theta)\\,d\\theta=2\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\cos\\theta\\cos(2m\\theta)\\,d\\theta=\\frac{2(-1)^{m-1}}{4m^2-1},$ where the last equality follows by integrating $\\frac12[\\cos((2m+1)\\theta)+\\cos((2m-1)\\theta)]$ using [F6]–[F7]. Multiplying by $2/\\pi$ gives $a_{2m}(f_s)=4(-1)^{m-1}/(\\pi(4m^2-1))$. This coefficient magnitude is asymptotic to $1/(\\pi m^2)$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
