    "ex-gaussian-and-simpson-weighted-mass-matrix-check": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "ex-two-node-gauss-legendre-exact-through-degree-three",
          "source_section": "Example",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice and take Lebesgue measure restricted to\nthe relative Borel sets of $[-1,1]$. Its relative support is all of $[-1,1]$,\nso it gives order-three moment data for $m=2$. The moments are\n$\\mu_0=2$, $\\mu_1=0$, $\\mu_2=2/3$, and $\\mu_3=0$. The monic orthogonal\npolynomials through degree two are $p_0=1$, $p_1=X$, and\n$p_2=X^2-1/3$. Thus the Gaussian nodes are\n$x_1=-1/\\sqrt3$ and $x_2=1/\\sqrt3$.\n\nThe Jacobi matrix is\n$$ J_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1/\\sqrt3\\\\1/\\sqrt3&0\\end{pmatrix}. $$\nIts unit eigenvectors $(1,-1)/\\sqrt2$ and $(1,1)/\\sqrt2$ give weights\n$w_1=w_2=\\mu_0/2=1$. Therefore\n$$ Q_2(f)=f(-1/\\sqrt3)+f(1/\\sqrt3) $$\nis exact for every real polynomial of degree at most three.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-legendre-weighted-derivative-orthogonality-and-norm",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $m,n\\ge0$, the polynomials of [[def-legendre-polynomial-family-and-l2-pairing]] satisfy\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^1P_n(x)P_m(x)\\,dx=\\frac{2}{2n+1}\\,\\delta_{mn},\n\\qquad\n\\int_{-1}^1(1-x^2)P_n'(x)P_m'(x)\\,dx=\\frac{2n(n+1)}{2n+1}\\,\\delta_{mn},\n$$\nwhere $\\delta_{mn}=1$ if $m=n$ and $0$ otherwise. Each $P_n$ has degree $n$ and nonzero leading coefficient, so $P_0,\\ldots,P_N$ form a basis of polynomials of degree at most $N$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "alg-gaussian-orthogonal-polynomial-galerkin-mass-matrix",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Input a degree $N\\ge0$, the monic orthogonal basis $p_0,\\ldots,p_N$ and the $(N+1)$-node Gaussian rule for the same positive measure $\\mu$ from [[def-gauss-quadrature-rule]]. With Gaussian nodes $\\xi_1,\\ldots,\\xi_{N+1}$ and weights $w_1,\\ldots,w_{N+1}$, output the matrix\n$$\nM_{ij}^{G}:=\\sum_{r=1}^{N+1}w_r p_i(\\xi_r)p_j(\\xi_r),\\qquad 0\\le i,j\\le N.\n$$\nThe inputs are admissible only when the moment data have order at least $2N+1$ and the rule nodes, weights, and basis all use the same measure. The algorithm performs the displayed finite exact-arithmetic sums; no floating-point conditioning claim is made.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-gaussian-orthogonal-polynomial-galerkin-mass-matrix-exact",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $N\\ge0$, take moment data of order $2N+1$ for a finite positive measure $\\mu$ with support containing at least $N+2$ points, and use its $(N+1)$-node Gaussian rule and orthogonal polynomials $p_0,\\ldots,p_N$. The matrix from [[alg-gaussian-orthogonal-polynomial-galerkin-mass-matrix]] has entries\n$$\nM_{ij}^{G}=\\int p_i(x)p_j(x)\\,d\\mu(x)=\n\\begin{cases}\n0,&i\\ne j,\\\\\nh_i,&i=j.\n\\end{cases}\n$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-chebyshev-arcsine-weighted-l2-pairing",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For continuous real functions $f,g$ on $[-1,1]$, define\n$$\n\\langle f,g\\rangle_C:=\\int_0^\\pi f(\\cos\\theta)g(\\cos\\theta)\\,d\\theta,\\qquad \\|f\\|_C^2:=\\langle f,f\\rangle_C.\n$$\nThis is the arcsine-weighted pairing on the compact Chebyshev interval: in the variable $x=\\cos\\theta$ its weight is $\\omega_C(x)=(1-x^2)^{-1/2}$ for $-1<x<1$. The angular integral is the endpoint convention, so no endpoint value of the singular density is required.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "alg-chebyshev-composite-simpson-weighted-mass-matrix",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Input a polynomial degree $N\\ge0$ and an even integer $M\\ge2$ specifying the number of uniform subintervals of $[0,\\pi]$. For $0\\le i,j\\le N$, form\n$$\ng_{ij}(\\theta)=T_i(\\cos\\theta)T_j(\\cos\\theta)\n$$\nand return\n$$\nM^{S}_{ij}:=Q_S(g_{ij};0,\\pi,M),\n$$\nwhere $Q_S$ is the composite Simpson procedure from [[alg-composite-simpson-rule]]. By the Chebyshev multiple-angle identity, $g_{ij}(\\theta)=\\cos(i\\theta)\\cos(j\\theta)$, so this is the composite-Simpson approximation of the corresponding arcsine-weighted mass entry. The procedure uses exact real arithmetic and asserts no quadrature or floating-point accuracy beyond a separately proved bound.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-composite-simpson-error-for-chebyshev-weighted-mass-matrix",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $N\\ge0$, let $M\\ge2$ be even, put $h=\\pi/M$, and let $G_{ij}=\\langle T_i,T_j\\rangle_C$ be the exact weighted mass entry from [[def-chebyshev-arcsine-weighted-l2-pairing]]. The approximation $M^S_{ij}$ from [[alg-chebyshev-composite-simpson-weighted-mass-matrix]] satisfies\n$$\n|G_{ij}-M^S_{ij}|\\le \\frac{\\pi h^4}{180}(i+j)^4,\\qquad 0\\le i,j\\le N.\n$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "lem-chebyshev-polynomials-orthogonal-in-arcsine-weight",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Under the pairing of [[def-chebyshev-arcsine-weighted-l2-pairing]],\n$$\n\\langle T_m,T_n\\rangle_C=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\pi,&m=n=0,\\\\\n\\pi/2,&m=n\\ge1,\\\\\n0,&m\\ne n.\n\\end{cases}\n$$\nThe polynomials $T_0,T_1,\\ldots$ form a basis of the real polynomial space, and the pairing is positive definite on continuous real functions on $[-1,1]$. If a continuous real function $h$ satisfies $\\langle h,T_n\\rangle_C=0$ for every $n\\ge0$, then $h=0$ on $[-1,1]$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$,\n$$\\sin(x+\\pi/2)=\\cos x,\\quad\\cos(x+\\pi/2)=-\\sin x,\\quad\\sin(x+\\pi)=-\\sin x,\\quad\\cos(x+\\pi)=-\\cos x.$$\nIn particular,\n$$\\sin(\\pi/2)=1,\\quad\\cos(\\pi/2)=0,\\quad\\sin\\pi=0,\\quad\\cos\\pi=-1.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-chebyshev-multiple-angle-identities",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every $n\\in\\mathbb N$ and real $\\theta$,\n$$T_n(\\cos\\theta)=\\cos(n\\theta),\\qquad U_n(\\cos\\theta)\\sin\\theta=\\sin((n+1)\\theta).$$ In particular, for $n\\ge1$ and $0\\le j\\le n$, $T_n(\\cos(j\\pi/n))=(-1)^j$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[def-chebyshev-polynomials-first-and-second-kind]], [[thm-sine-and-cosine-addition-formulas]], [[cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity]], [[thm-induction-principle]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Under [A1], the explicit Gaussian rule [F1] applies. Its sum for the basis $1,x$ is $\\begin{pmatrix}1&-1/\\sqrt3\\\\1&1/\\sqrt3\\end{pmatrix}^{\\!T} \\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix}1&-1/\\sqrt3\\\\1&1/\\sqrt3\\end{pmatrix} =\\begin{pmatrix}2&0\\\\0&2/3\\end{pmatrix}.$ The entries agree with exact integrals by [F2], as also asserted by [F3].",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "By [F9], $g_{00}=1$, $g_{01}=\\cos\\theta$, and $g_{11}=\\cos^2\\theta$. Substitution of the quarter-turn values [F8] into [F5] gives $M^S_{00}=\\pi$, $M^S_{01}=0$, and $M^S_{11}=\\pi/3$. The exact mass matrix is $G=\\operatorname{diag}(\\pi,\\pi/2)$ by [F7].",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The only nonzero entry error is $|G_{11}-M^S_{11}|=\\pi/6$. The Simpson theorem [F6] applies with $M=2$, $h=\\pi/2$, and $i=j=1$, and gives its bound $\\pi^5/180$. Therefore the displayed finite calculation matches the general entrywise estimate.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F6",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
